<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-a"><h2>A</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="abacus"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/abacus">Abacus</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Counting frame)</span></dt><dd>An abacus, or counting frame, is a hand-operated calculating tool of beads or counters moved on a board or rods, first known in Sumer, where it appeared between 2700 and 2300 BCE with columns for the orders of magnitude of the base-60…</dd></div><div id="abo-blood-group-system"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/abo-blood-group-system">ABO blood group system</a> <span class="gl-aka">(ABO blood types)</span></dt><dd>The ABO blood group system classifies human blood by the presence of the A and B antigens on red blood cells; the Austrian physician Karl Landsteiner discovered it at the University of Vienna, dividing blood into three groups in 1901…</dd></div><div id="abul-abbas"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-abbas">Abul-Abbas</a></dt><dd>Abul-Abbas was an Asian elephant sent by the Abbasid caliph Harun al-Rashid to Charlemagne.</dd></div><div id="acupuncture"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/acupuncture">Acupuncture</a></dt><dd>Acupuncture is a practice of traditional Chinese medicine in which thin needles are inserted into the body, believed to have originated in China around 100 BCE, when the Huangdi Neijing first documented a system of diagnosis and treatment…</dd></div><div id="adinkra"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/adinkra">Adinkra</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Adinkra symbols)</span></dt><dd>Adinkra are visual symbols of the Akan peoples of Ghana, each representing a concept, proverb or value.</dd></div><div id="advocate-of-the-holy-sepulchre"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/advocate-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Advocate of the Holy Sepulchre</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Advocatus Sancti Sepulchri)</span></dt><dd>Advocate of the Holy Sepulchre was the title taken by Godfrey of Bouillon after the crusaders captured Jerusalem in 1099, when he declined to be called king.</dd></div><div id="aeolipile"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/aeolipile">Aeolipile</a> <span class="gl-aka">(aeolipyle, eolipile)</span></dt><dd>An aeolipile, or Hero's engine, is a simple bladeless steam turbine, a vessel that spins as steam escapes through bent nozzles, described by Hero of Alexandria in the 1st century CE and usually credited to him.</dd></div><div id="aerial-bombardment"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/aerial-bombardment">Aerial bombardment</a></dt><dd>Aerial bombardment is the use of aircraft to drop bombs or other munitions on military or civilian targets.</dd></div><div id="aeroplane"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/aeroplane">Aeroplane</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Airplane)</span></dt><dd>An aeroplane is a fixed-wing aircraft driven forward by the thrust of a propeller, jet engine or rocket engine; the Wright brothers invented and flew the Wright Flyer in 1903, in what is recognised as the first sustained and controlled…</dd></div><div id="afrikaans"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a></dt><dd>Afrikaans is a West Germanic language that developed at the Cape of Good Hope from the Dutch spoken by settlers of the Dutch East India Company, with influence from Malay, Portuguese, Khoekhoe and other languages.</dd></div><div id="agent-orange"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/agent-orange">Agent Orange</a></dt><dd>Agent Orange was a herbicide sprayed by the United States military over forests and crops in South Vietnam, Laos and Cambodia during the Vietnam War, mainly between 1962 and 1971.</dd></div><div id="agora"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/agora">Agora</a></dt><dd>An agora was the central public space of an ancient Greek city, used for markets, assemblies and civic life.</dd></div><div id="aircraft-carrier"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/aircraft-carrier">Aircraft carrier</a> <span class="gl-aka">(fleet carriers)</span></dt><dd>An aircraft carrier is a warship designed to carry, launch and recover aircraft, serving as a mobile air base at sea.</dd></div><div id="ajaw"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ajaw">Ajaw</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Ahau)</span></dt><dd>Ajaw, 'lord', is a Maya political title known from pre-Columbian inscriptions, used for any of the leading nobles of a polity.</dd></div><div id="akkadian-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-language">Akkadian language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Akkadian)</span></dt><dd>Akkadian is an extinct Semitic language of ancient Mesopotamia, written in cuneiform.</dd></div><div id="aku-uka"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/aku-uka">Aku Uka</a></dt><dd>Aku Uka is the title of the sacred king of the Jukun people of the Benue valley in Nigeria.</dd></div><div id="akuapem-twi"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/akuapem-twi">Akuapem Twi</a></dt><dd>Akuapem Twi is a dialect of the Akan language spoken on the Akuapem ridge in south-eastern Ghana.</dd></div><div id="alake-of-egbaland"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/alake-of-egbaland">Alake of Egbaland</a></dt><dd>The Alake of Egbaland is the paramount traditional ruler of the Egba, a Yoruba people whose capital is Abeokuta in south-western Nigeria.</dd></div><div id="alanic-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/alanic-language">Alanic language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Alanic)</span></dt><dd>Alanic was the Eastern Iranian language of the Alans, a nomadic people of the steppes north of the Caucasus in late antiquity and the Middle Ages.</dd></div><div id="alchemy"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/alchemy">Alchemy</a></dt><dd>Alchemy is a philosophical and protoscientific tradition whose common aims were turning base metals into gold and making an elixir of immortality; in its Western form it is first attested in texts written in Greco-Roman Egypt in the first…</dd></div><div id="amanyanabo"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/amanyanabo">Amanyanabo</a></dt><dd>Amanyanabo, meaning owner of the town, is the title of the traditional rulers of the Ijaw city-states of the eastern Niger Delta, such as Kalabari, Bonny, Nembe and Okrika.</dd></div><div id="amarna-period"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/amarna-period">Amarna Period</a></dt><dd>The Amarna Period is the late Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt in the mid-fourteenth century BCE, named after Amarna (Akhetaten), the new capital built by the pharaoh Akhenaten for the cult of the sun disc Aten.</dd></div><div id="amharic"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Amharic language)</span></dt><dd>Amharic is a Semitic language of Ethiopia, written in the Ge'ez script, and the working language of the Ethiopian federal government.</dd></div><div id="amphora"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/amphora">Amphora</a> <span class="gl-aka">(amphorae)</span></dt><dd>An amphora is a two-handled ceramic jar used in the ancient Mediterranean world to transport and store goods such as wine and olive oil.</dd></div><div id="analytical-engine"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/analytical-engine">Analytical Engine</a></dt><dd>The Analytical Engine is a design for a mechanical general-purpose computer by the English mathematician Charles Babbage, first described in 1837 as the successor to his difference engine.</dd></div><div id="anden"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/anden">Andén</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Andenes)</span></dt><dd>An andén (plural andenes) is a stair-step terrace dug into a hillside for farming, built by pre-Columbian cultures in the Andes by 2000 BCE.</dd></div><div id="ankole-watusi"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ankole-watusi">Ankole-Watusi</a></dt><dd>The Ankole-Watusi is a breed of cattle known for its enormous horns, developed in the United States in the twentieth century from long-horned Sanga cattle of east-central Africa.</dd></div><div id="antillean-creole"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/antillean-creole">Antillean Creole</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Kwéyòl, Patwa)</span></dt><dd>Antillean Creole is a French-based creole language spoken in the Lesser Antilles, including Martinique, Guadeloupe, Dominica and Saint Lucia, where it is called Kwéyòl.</dd></div><div id="antiseptic-surgery"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/antiseptic-surgery">Antiseptic surgery</a></dt><dd>Antiseptic surgery is the use in operations of antiseptics, substances applied to living tissue to reduce the risk of infection; some French surgeons adopted carbolic acid for it about a decade after Ignaz Semmelweis showed, in 1847–1848…</dd></div><div id="aqueduct"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">Aqueduct</a> <span class="gl-aka">(aqueducts)</span></dt><dd>An aqueduct is a channel or structure built to carry water from a source to a city, often across valleys on arches.</dd></div><div id="aquifer"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/aquifer">Aquifer</a></dt><dd>An aquifer is an underground layer of rock or sediment that holds water.</dd></div><div id="arabic-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/arabic-language">Arabic language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Arabic)</span></dt><dd>Arabic is a Semitic language that spread with Islam from the Arabian Peninsula across the Middle East and North Africa.</dd></div><div id="aramaic"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a></dt><dd>Aramaic is a Semitic language that became the common language of much of the Near East in the first millennium BCE.</dd></div><div id="arawakan-languages"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/arawakan-languages">Arawakan languages</a></dt><dd>The Arawakan languages are a family of languages of South America and the Caribbean, spoken from the Andes and Amazonia to the Antilles.</dd></div><div id="arch"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/arch">Arch</a></dt><dd>An arch is a curved structure spanning an opening, bearing the load above it or serving as decoration, first known in the ancient Near East, where the Nippur arch was built before 3800 BCE.</dd></div><div id="arch-marshal-of-the-holy-roman-empire"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/arch-marshal-of-the-holy-roman-empire">Arch-Marshal of the Holy Roman Empire</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Erzmarschall)</span></dt><dd>The Arch-Marshal (Erzmarschall) was one of the ceremonial high offices of the Holy Roman Empire attached to the electoral dignity.</dd></div><div id="archery"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/archery">Archery</a> <span class="gl-aka">(archers)</span></dt><dd>Archery is the use of bows to shoot arrows in hunting and warfare.</dd></div><div id="area-of-outstanding-natural-beauty"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/area-of-outstanding-natural-beauty">Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty</a> <span class="gl-aka">(AONB)</span></dt><dd>An Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONB) is a protected landscape designation in England, Wales and Northern Ireland, created under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act 1949.</dd></div><div id="arpanet"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/arpanet">ARPANET</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Advanced Research Projects Agency Network)</span></dt><dd>The ARPANET (Advanced Research Projects Agency Network) was the first wide-area packet-switched network with distributed control, set up by the United States Department of Defense; its first host-to-host connection, between UCLA and the…</dd></div><div id="arquebus"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/arquebus">Arquebus</a></dt><dd>The arquebus was an early form of matchlock firearm used from the fifteenth to the seventeenth century.</dd></div><div id="arras-culture"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/arras-culture">Arras culture</a></dt><dd>The Arras culture is an Iron Age culture of East Yorkshire in England, from about the 4th century BCE to the Roman conquest, named after a cemetery at Arras near Market Weighton.</dd></div><div id="artemisinin"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/artemisinin">Artemisinin</a> <span class="gl-aka">(qinghaosu)</span></dt><dd>Artemisinin is a drug against malaria caused by Plasmodium falciparum, discovered in China in 1972 by Tu Youyou in the leaves of sweet wormwood (Artemisia annua).</dd></div><div id="arung-matoa"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/arung-matoa">Arung Matoa</a></dt><dd>Arung Matoa, roughly &quot;chief lord&quot; in Bugis, was the title of the head of the Bugis kingdom of Wajo in South Sulawesi.</dd></div><div id="ashura"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ashura">Ashura</a></dt><dd>Ashura is the tenth day of Muharram, the first month of the Islamic calendar.</dd></div><div id="asomtavruli"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/asomtavruli">Asomtavruli</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Mrgvlovani)</span></dt><dd>Asomtavruli is the oldest form of the Georgian alphabet, written with rounded letters.</dd></div><div id="aspirin"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/aspirin">Aspirin</a> <span class="gl-aka">(acetylsalicylic acid)</span></dt><dd>Aspirin is acetylsalicylic acid, a drug that reduces pain, fever and inflammation and helps prevent blood clots; the chemist Charles Frédéric Gerhardt first produced it in 1853, and Felix Hoffmann (or perhaps Arthur Eichengrün) of the…</dd></div><div id="associates-of-the-swiss-confederacy"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/associates-of-the-swiss-confederacy">Associates of the Swiss Confederacy</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Zugewandte Orte)</span></dt><dd>The Associates of the Swiss Confederacy, in German zugewandte Orte, were territories allied to the Old Swiss Confederacy without being full member cantons.</dd></div><div id="astrolabe"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/astrolabe">Astrolabe</a></dt><dd>An astrolabe is an astronomical instrument that serves as a star chart and model of the visible sky, used to measure the altitude of stars and find latitude; it dates from classical antiquity, and Theon of Alexandria (c. 335–405) wrote a…</dd></div><div id="atlantic-slave-trade"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Atlantic trade, slave trade)</span></dt><dd>The Atlantic slave trade was the forced transportation of millions of enslaved Africans to the Americas between the sixteenth and nineteenth centuries.</dd></div><div id="attic-standard"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/attic-standard">Attic standard</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Attic silver standard)</span></dt><dd>The Attic standard was the weight standard for silver coinage originating in Athens, based on a tetradrachm of about 17 grams.</dd></div><div id="attrition"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/attrition">Attrition</a></dt><dd>Attrition refers to a form of warfare in which victory depends on wearing down the enemy's manpower and material resources through continuous losses.</dd></div><div id="augustal-prefect"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/augustal-prefect">Augustal prefect</a> <span class="gl-aka">(praefectus augustalis)</span></dt><dd>The Augustal prefect (praefectus augustalis) was the title of the governor of Egypt in the late Roman Empire.</dd></div><div id="automobile"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/automobile">Automobile</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Motor car)</span></dt><dd>An automobile, or car, is a wheeled motor vehicle that runs mainly on roads; the modern car was invented in 1886, when the German inventor Carl Benz patented his Benz Patent-Motorwagen.</dd></div><div id="awoamefia"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/awoamefia">Awoamefia</a></dt><dd>Awoamefia is the title of the paramount ruler of the Anlo Ewe of south-eastern Ghana, who resides at Anloga.</dd></div><div id="awujale"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/awujale">Awujale</a></dt><dd>The Awujale is the title of the paramount ruler of the Ijebu Kingdom, a Yoruba state in south-western Nigeria with its seat at Ijebu-Ode.</dd></div><div id="ayllu"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ayllu">Ayllu</a></dt><dd>The ayllu is the traditional community of the Andes, especially among the Quechua and Aymara: a network of families, often with a putative or fictive common ancestor, holding a territory in common.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-b"><h2>B</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="bakelite"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/bakelite">Bakelite</a></dt><dd>Bakelite is a hard phenol formaldehyde resin, the first plastic made from synthetic components, developed by the chemist Leo Baekeland in Yonkers, New York, in 1907 and patented in 1909.</dd></div><div id="balhara"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/balhara">Balhara</a></dt><dd>Balhara was the name by which Arab travellers and geographers of the ninth and tenth centuries, such as Sulaiman the Merchant, Ibn Khordadbeh and al-Masudi, referred to the Rashtrakuta kings of the Deccan.</dd></div><div id="balochi-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/balochi-language">Balochi language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Balochi)</span></dt><dd>Balochi is an Iranian language spoken by the Baloch people in Pakistan's Balochistan province, south-eastern Iran, southern Afghanistan and among communities in Oman and the Gulf.</dd></div><div id="bantustan"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">Bantustan</a> <span class="gl-aka">(homeland)</span></dt><dd>A Bantustan, or homeland, was a territory set aside for Black South Africans of a particular ethnic group under apartheid.</dd></div><div id="battering-ram"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/battering-ram">Battering ram</a> <span class="gl-aka">(battering rams)</span></dt><dd>A battering ram is a siege weapon consisting of a heavy beam used to break down gates and walls.</dd></div><div id="bazaar"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">Bazaar</a> <span class="gl-aka">(bazaars)</span></dt><dd>A bazaar is a market or commercial quarter, typically of covered streets, shops and workshops, found in cities across the Islamic world and South Asia.</dd></div><div id="ba-shu-script"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ba-shu-script">Ba–Shu script</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Ba-Shu symbols)</span></dt><dd>The Ba–Shu script is a set of undeciphered signs and symbols found on bronze weapons, seals and other objects of the ancient Ba and Shu cultures of Sichuan and Chongqing, dating from about the 5th to 3rd centuries BCE.</dd></div><div id="bead"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/bead">Bead</a> <span class="gl-aka">(beads)</span></dt><dd>Beads are small decorative objects of glass, stone, shell or metal, pierced for threading, which served widely as trade goods and stores of value.</dd></div><div id="beer"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/beer">Beer</a></dt><dd>Beer is one of the oldest human-produced drinks, made by fermenting cereal grains; the earliest archaeological evidence of fermentation is 13,000-year-old residues of a gruel-like beer used by the Natufians for ritual feasting at Raqefet…</dd></div><div id="benzoin-resin"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/benzoin-resin">Benzoin resin</a> <span class="gl-aka">(benzoin)</span></dt><dd>Benzoin resin is an aromatic balsamic resin obtained from trees of the genus Styrax, used as incense, in perfume and in medicine.</dd></div><div id="berber-languages"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/berber-languages">Berber languages</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Tamazight, Amazigh languages)</span></dt><dd>The Berber languages, also called Tamazight, are a branch of the Afroasiatic family spoken by the Amazigh peoples of North Africa, from Morocco and Algeria to Libya, Egypt's Siwa Oasis and the Tuareg regions of the Sahara and Sahel.</dd></div><div id="bessemer-process"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/bessemer-process">Bessemer process</a></dt><dd>The Bessemer process makes steel from molten pig iron by blowing air through it to burn off excess carbon and other impurities; it was patented by the Englishman Henry Bessemer in 1856.</dd></div><div id="biatec"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/biatec">Biatec</a></dt><dd>Biatec is a name, probably that of a Celtic ruler, inscribed on large silver coins minted in the 1st century BCE at the oppidum on the site of modern Bratislava, in the lands of the Boii.</dd></div><div id="bicameral-legislature"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/bicameral-legislature">Bicameral legislature</a></dt><dd>A bicameral legislature is a lawmaking body divided into two separate chambers or houses.</dd></div><div id="bicycle"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/bicycle">Bicycle</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Pedal cycle, Push-bike)</span></dt><dd>A bicycle is a pedal-driven, single-track vehicle with two wheels attached to a frame, one behind the other.</dd></div><div id="black-ships"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/black-ships">Black Ships</a></dt><dd>The Black Ships was the Japanese name for the Western vessels, especially the steam-powered warships of Commodore Matthew Perry's American squadron that entered Edo Bay in 1853.</dd></div><div id="blue-riband"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/blue-riband">Blue Riband</a></dt><dd>The Blue Riband is an unofficial honour given to the passenger liner making the fastest regular crossing of the Atlantic.</dd></div><div id="bokmal"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/bokmal">Bokmål</a></dt><dd>Bokmål is the more widely used of the two official written standards of Norwegian, alongside Nynorsk.</dd></div><div id="bombard"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/bombard">Bombard</a> <span class="gl-aka">(bombards)</span></dt><dd>A bombard is a large early cannon, usually a muzzle-loader firing heavy stone balls, used mainly in sieges in the fourteenth to sixteenth centuries.</dd></div><div id="bonnie-blue-flag"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/bonnie-blue-flag">Bonnie Blue Flag</a></dt><dd>The Bonnie Blue Flag is a flag of a single white star on a blue field, first flown by the short-lived Republic of West Florida in 1810.</dd></div><div id="borbarua"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/borbarua">Borbarua</a></dt><dd>The Borbarua was a high office of the Ahom kingdom of Assam, created by King Pratap Singha in the early 17th century.</dd></div><div id="borgohain"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/borgohain">Borgohain</a></dt><dd>The Borgohain was one of the two oldest ministerial offices of the Ahom kingdom of Assam, alongside the Burhagohain, traditionally dated to the founder Sukaphaa in the 13th century.</dd></div><div id="borpatrogohain"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/borpatrogohain">Borpatrogohain</a></dt><dd>The Borpatrogohain was a ministerial office of the Ahom kingdom of Assam, created by King Suhungmung in the early 16th century as a third great counsellor beside the Burhagohain and the Borgohain.</dd></div><div id="brahmi-script"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/brahmi-script">Brahmi script</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Brāhmī)</span></dt><dd>Brahmi is a writing system of ancient India that appeared as a fully developed script in the 3rd century BCE; its earliest securely dated inscriptions are the rock-cut edicts of Ashoka, from 250–232 BCE.</dd></div><div id="brak"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/brak">Brak</a></dt><dd>Brak was the title of the kings of Waalo, a Wolof kingdom on the lower Senegal river.</dd></div><div id="brazilwood"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/brazilwood">Brazilwood</a></dt><dd>Brazilwood is a tropical tree whose dense red heartwood was valued in early modern Europe as the source of a red dye for textiles.</dd></div><div id="breech-loading-rifle"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/breech-loading-rifle">Breech-loading rifle</a> <span class="gl-aka">(breech-loading rifles)</span></dt><dd>A breech-loading rifle is a firearm loaded at the rear of the barrel rather than through the muzzle, allowing much faster reloading, especially when lying down.</dd></div><div id="bren-light-machine-gun"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/bren-light-machine-gun">Bren light machine gun</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Bren gun)</span></dt><dd>The Bren light machine gun was the standard light machine gun of the British and Commonwealth armies in the Second World War.</dd></div><div id="brigade"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> <span class="gl-aka">(brigades)</span></dt><dd>A brigade is a military formation made up of several battalions or regiments, smaller than a division.</dd></div><div id="british-overseas-territories"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/british-overseas-territories">British Overseas Territories</a> <span class="gl-aka">(British Overseas Territory)</span></dt><dd>The British Overseas Territories are the fourteen territories under British sovereignty that are not part of the United Kingdom, mostly small islands left from the British Empire, such as Gibraltar, the Falkland Islands, Montserrat and…</dd></div><div id="broch"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/broch">Broch</a></dt><dd>A broch is a type of Iron Age drystone tower found in northern and western Scotland, especially in Caithness, Orkney and Shetland.</dd></div><div id="bronze"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> <span class="gl-aka">(bronze industry)</span></dt><dd>Bronze is an alloy of copper and tin, and the term is also used loosely for related copper alloys such as brass.</dd></div><div id="bronze-casting"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/bronze-casting">Bronze casting</a> <span class="gl-aka">(bronze-casting)</span></dt><dd>Bronze casting is the technique of shaping objects by pouring molten copper alloy into moulds, often using the lost-wax method.</dd></div><div id="bronze-working"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/bronze-working">Bronze-working</a></dt><dd>Bronze-working is the craft of producing tools, weapons and ornaments from bronze, an alloy of copper and tin.</dd></div><div id="bukhar-khudah"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/bukhar-khudah">Bukhar Khudah</a></dt><dd>Bukhar Khudah, 'lord of Bukhara', was the title of the local rulers of Bukhara before and during the Arab conquest of Transoxiana.</dd></div><div id="bull-leaping"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/bull-leaping">Bull-leaping</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Taurokathapsia)</span></dt><dd>Bull-leaping is an acrobatic feat in which a performer vaults over a charging bull, shown above all in the art of Minoan Crete, where it is thought to have been a key ritual of the elite and perhaps of religion.</dd></div><div id="bumiputera"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/bumiputera">Bumiputera</a></dt><dd>Bumiputera, 'sons of the soil', is the Malaysian term for the Malays and the indigenous peoples of the country, including the Orang Asli and the peoples of Sabah and Sarawak.</dd></div><div id="burhagohain"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/burhagohain">Burhagohain</a></dt><dd>The Burhagohain was the senior of the great ministers of the Ahom kingdom of Assam, an office traditionally dated to the reign of the founder Sukaphaa in the 13th century.</dd></div><div id="burushaski"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/burushaski">Burushaski</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Burushaski language)</span></dt><dd>Burushaski is a language spoken in the Hunza, Nagar and Yasin valleys of Gilgit-Baltistan in northern Pakistan.</dd></div><div id="buurba-jolof"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/buurba-jolof">Buurba Jolof</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Burba Jolof)</span></dt><dd>Buurba Jolof was the title of the kings of the Jolof Empire, who were overlords of the Wolof states of Senegal.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-c"><h2>C</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="cacao"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cacao">Cacao</a></dt><dd>Cacao is the tree native to tropical Mesoamerica whose beans are used to make chocolate.</dd></div><div id="cadastral-survey"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cadastral-survey">Cadastral survey</a> <span class="gl-aka">(cadastral)</span></dt><dd>A cadastral survey is a systematic record of land parcels, recording their area, ownership, use and value, usually for taxation.</dd></div><div id="calabash"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/calabash">Calabash</a></dt><dd>A calabash is a gourd dried and hollowed out for use as a container.</dd></div><div id="calligraphy"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">Calligraphy</a></dt><dd>Calligraphy is the art of decorative handwriting, highly developed in the Islamic world, where it adorned manuscripts, buildings and objects.</dd></div><div id="camel"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/camel">Camel</a></dt><dd>The camel is a large hoofed mammal adapted to arid environments, used for transport, milk, meat and warfare.</dd></div><div id="camel-warfare"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/camel-warfare">Camel warfare</a></dt><dd>Camel warfare refers to the use of camels in combat, for transport of troops and supplies or as mounts.</dd></div><div id="camera-obscura"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/camera-obscura">Camera obscura</a></dt><dd>A camera obscura is a dark chamber or box in which light passing through a small hole projects an inverted image of the scene outside onto the opposite surface; one of the earliest known written records of the effect is the Chinese text…</dd></div><div id="camphor"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/camphor">Camphor</a></dt><dd>Camphor is a fragrant crystalline substance obtained from trees, prized in Asia as a medicine, perfume and ritual material.</dd></div><div id="canadian-red-ensign"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/canadian-red-ensign">Canadian Red Ensign</a></dt><dd>The Canadian Red Ensign was a red flag with the Union Jack in the canton and the Canadian coat of arms in the fly, used as Canada's unofficial and later official national flag on land and at sea from the late nineteenth century.</dd></div><div id="cannon"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cannon">Cannon</a></dt><dd>A cannon is a large-calibre gun, a type of artillery firing a projectile with an explosive propellant, first securely attested in China in the 13th century.</dd></div><div id="cape-dutch"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-dutch">Cape Dutch</a></dt><dd>Cape Dutch was the form of Dutch spoken at the Cape of Good Hope by settlers, the enslaved and Khoekhoe communities from the 17th century.</dd></div><div id="cape-verdean-creole"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-verdean-creole">Cape Verdean Creole</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Kriolu, Kabuverdianu)</span></dt><dd>Cape Verdean Creole, known as Kriolu, is a Portuguese-based creole language spoken in Cape Verde and by its emigrant communities.</dd></div><div id="caravan-trade"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">Caravan trade</a> <span class="gl-aka">(caravan routes, caravan commerce)</span></dt><dd>Caravan trade is long-distance overland commerce carried by groups of merchants with pack animals, typically camels, travelling together for safety.</dd></div><div id="caravanserai"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">Caravanserai</a></dt><dd>A caravanserai is a roadside inn built around a courtyard, providing lodging for travellers and their animals along trade routes in the Islamic world.</dd></div><div id="caravel"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/caravel">Caravel</a></dt><dd>A caravel was a small, highly manoeuvrable sailing ship, often with lateen sails that allowed it to sail close to the wind.</dd></div><div id="carolingian-minuscule"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-minuscule">Carolingian minuscule</a></dt><dd>Carolingian minuscule is a clear, legible script developed in the late eighth century in the Carolingian Empire.</dd></div><div id="carrack"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/carrack">Carrack</a></dt><dd>A carrack was a large, three- or four-masted ocean-going sailing ship developed in the Mediterranean and Iberia in the fifteenth century.</dd></div><div id="cartography"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cartography">Cartography</a></dt><dd>Cartography is the science and art of making maps.</dd></div><div id="cassava"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cassava">Cassava</a></dt><dd>Cassava, also called manioc, is a starchy root crop native to South America.</dd></div><div id="cast-iron"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cast-iron">Cast iron</a></dt><dd>Cast iron is an iron–carbon alloy with more than 2% carbon, valued for its relatively low melting temperature; the earliest known cast-iron artefacts, from what is now Jiangsu in China, date to the 8th century BCE.</dd></div><div id="casta"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/casta">Casta</a></dt><dd>Casta, Spanish and Portuguese for 'lineage', was a label of descent and standing in colonial Spanish America, chiefly for people of mixed Spanish, Indigenous and African ancestry.</dd></div><div id="caste"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/caste">Caste</a></dt><dd>Caste refers to a system of hereditary social groups, often tied to occupation and restricting marriage between groups.</dd></div><div id="cataphract"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cataphract">Cataphract</a> <span class="gl-aka">(cataphracts)</span></dt><dd>A cataphract was a heavily armoured cavalryman, with both rider and horse protected by armour, who charged with a long lance.</dd></div><div id="catapult"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/catapult">Catapult</a> <span class="gl-aka">(catapults)</span></dt><dd>A catapult is a siege engine that hurls projectiles using tension, torsion or human traction, used before gunpowder artillery.</dd></div><div id="cataract-surgery"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cataract-surgery">Cataract surgery</a></dt><dd>Cataract surgery is the treatment of the clouding of the eye's lens.</dd></div><div id="cattle"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cattle">Cattle</a></dt><dd>Cattle are domesticated bovines kept for milk, meat, hides and draught, and in many societies as a measure of wealth.</dd></div><div id="census"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/census">Census</a></dt><dd>A census is an official count of a population, often recording households, property and obligations.</dd></div><div id="cfa-franc"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cfa-franc">CFA franc</a> <span class="gl-aka">(West African CFA franc, Central African CFA franc)</span></dt><dd>The CFA franc is the name of two currencies used in West and Central Africa, created by France in December 1945 for its African colonies.</dd></div><div id="chagatai-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-language">Chagatai language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Chaghatai Turkic)</span></dt><dd>Chagatai is an extinct Turkic literary language of Central Asia, named after the Chagatai Khanate.</dd></div><div id="chain-mail"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/chain-mail">Chain mail</a> <span class="gl-aka">(chain-mail)</span></dt><dd>Chain mail is armour made of small interlocking metal rings.</dd></div><div id="chalukya-vikrama-era"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-vikrama-era">Chalukya-Vikrama era</a></dt><dd>The Chalukya-Vikrama era was a calendar era introduced by the Western Chalukya king Vikramaditya VI to count years from his accession in 1076.</dd></div><div id="champa-rice"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/champa-rice">Champa rice</a></dt><dd>Champa rice refers to fast-ripening, drought-resistant rice varieties introduced to China from Champa in present-day Vietnam.</dd></div><div id="chao-currency"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/chao-currency">Chao (currency)</a></dt><dd>Chao was the paper currency issued by the Yuan Dynasty in China.</dd></div><div id="charcoal"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">Charcoal</a></dt><dd>Charcoal is a fuel produced by heating wood in the absence of air, and was essential for smelting iron in the pre-industrial world.</dd></div><div id="chariot"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/chariot">Chariot</a></dt><dd>A chariot is a light, two-wheeled, horse-drawn vehicle used in warfare, carrying a driver and an archer or spearman.</dd></div><div id="chemical-weapons"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons">Chemical weapons</a></dt><dd>Chemical weapons are toxic substances used in war to kill or injure, such as mustard gas.</dd></div><div id="chernozem"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/chernozem">Chernozem</a> <span class="gl-aka">(black-earth)</span></dt><dd>Chernozem, or black earth, is a highly fertile dark soil rich in humus, found across the steppes of Ukraine and southern Russia.</dd></div><div id="chhau-dance"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/chhau-dance">Chhau dance</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Chhau)</span></dt><dd>Chhau is a tradition of martial and folk dance from eastern India, performed in three main styles, Purulia in West Bengal, Seraikela in Jharkhand and Mayurbhanj in Odisha.</dd></div><div id="chinampa"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/chinampa">Chinampa</a> <span class="gl-aka">(chinampas)</span></dt><dd>A chinampa is an artificial agricultural plot built up from mud and vegetation in shallow lake beds, separated by canals.</dd></div><div id="chu-nom"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/chu-nom">Chữ Nôm</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Nôm, Chu Nom)</span></dt><dd>Chữ Nôm is a script for writing the Vietnamese language, built from Chinese characters and new characters formed on their model.</dd></div><div id="cinematograph"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cinematograph">Cinematograph</a> <span class="gl-aka">(kinematograph, Cinématographe)</span></dt><dd>The cinematograph is an early motion-picture machine that served as both film camera and projector; the French inventor Léon Bouly coined the name in a patent of 12 February 1892, and filed for a version that could also project in…</dd></div><div id="cinnamon"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cinnamon">Cinnamon</a></dt><dd>Cinnamon is a spice made from the inner bark of trees of the genus Cinnamomum, the finest coming from Sri Lanka.</dd></div><div id="cipollino-marble"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cipollino-marble">Cipollino marble</a> <span class="gl-aka">(marmor Carystium)</span></dt><dd>Cipollino is a green-and-white banded marble quarried in antiquity near Karystos in southern Euboea, Greece.</dd></div><div id="cis-sutlej-states"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cis-sutlej-states">Cis-Sutlej states</a></dt><dd>The Cis-Sutlej states were the Sikh and other states of the Malwa region of Punjab between the Sutlej and Yamuna rivers, among them Patiala, Nabha and Jind.</dd></div><div id="classical-greece"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/classical-greece">Classical Greece</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Classical period of ancient Greece)</span></dt><dd>Classical Greece is the period of ancient Greek history from the Persian Wars of the early fifth century BCE to the death of Alexander the Great in 323 BCE.</dd></div><div id="clay-tokens"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/clay-tokens">Clay tokens</a></dt><dd>Clay tokens are small shaped pieces of clay that stood for animals and goods, first used to keep records in the ancient Near East around 8000–7500 BCE; the earliest known come from the Zagros region of Iran.</dd></div><div id="clear-script"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/clear-script">Clear script</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Todo bichig)</span></dt><dd>The Clear script (todo bichig) is an alphabet created in 1648 by the Oirat monk Zaya Pandita, adapted from the traditional Mongolian script to write the Oirat language more exactly.</dd></div><div id="cloves"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cloves">Cloves</a></dt><dd>Cloves are the aromatic dried flower buds of a tree native to the Maluku Islands of Indonesia.</dd></div><div id="coal"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a></dt><dd>Coal is a combustible sedimentary rock used as fuel.</dd></div><div id="codex"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/codex">Codex</a></dt><dd>A codex is a book of pages stacked and bound at one edge, the ancestor of the modern book, first described in the 1st century CE, when the Roman poet Martial praised its convenience.</dd></div><div id="coffee-economy"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">Coffee economy</a> <span class="gl-aka">(coffee)</span></dt><dd>Coffee is a drink made from the roasted seeds of the coffee plant, originally from Ethiopia and Yemen, which spread through the Islamic world and then Europe.</dd></div><div id="cog-ship"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cog-ship">Cog (ship)</a></dt><dd>The cog was a type of sailing ship used in northern Europe from the 10th to the 14th century, with a flat bottom, high straight sides, a single square sail and, later, a stern rudder and raised castles.</dd></div><div id="cohort"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cohort">Cohort</a></dt><dd>A cohort was a tactical unit of the Roman army, normally about 480 men, with ten cohorts forming a legion.</dd></div><div id="coinage"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/coinage">Coinage</a></dt><dd>Coinage is the minting of small metal pieces for use as money, first known in the 7th century BCE in China, where a factory at Guanzhuang in Henan made shovel-shaped bronze coins between 640 and 550 BCE, and in Anatolia, where the…</dd></div><div id="columbian-exchange"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/columbian-exchange">Columbian exchange</a> <span class="gl-aka">(New World crops)</span></dt><dd>The Columbian Exchange is the transfer of plants, animals, diseases and people between the Americas and the rest of the world following Columbus's voyages from 1492.</dd></div><div id="companions-of-the-prophet"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/companions-of-the-prophet">Companions of the Prophet</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Sahaba)</span></dt><dd>The Companions of the Prophet, in Arabic sahaba, are the people who knew or saw the Prophet Muhammad and believed in him.</dd></div><div id="composite-bow"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/composite-bow">Composite bow</a></dt><dd>A composite bow is a bow made from layers of wood, horn and sinew glued together, giving great power in a short length suitable for use on horseback.</dd></div><div id="concentration-camp"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">Concentration camp</a></dt><dd>A concentration camp is a place where large numbers of civilians or prisoners are detained, usually without trial, in harsh conditions.</dd></div><div id="condolence-ceremony"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/condolence-ceremony">Condolence Ceremony</a></dt><dd>The Condolence Ceremony is the central ritual of the Haudenosaunee (Iroquois) Confederacy, held to mourn a dead chief and to raise up his successor.</dd></div><div id="congreve-rocket"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/congreve-rocket">Congreve rocket</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Congreve rockets)</span></dt><dd>The Congreve rocket was a British military rocket developed by William Congreve in the early nineteenth century, based on rockets used in Mysore.</dd></div><div id="conscription"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/conscription">Conscription</a> <span class="gl-aka">(conscript)</span></dt><dd>Conscription is compulsory enlistment of people into state service, especially the armed forces.</dd></div><div id="constitutional-monarchy"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy</a></dt><dd>A constitutional monarchy is a form of government in which a monarch is head of state but exercises power within the limits of a constitution.</dd></div><div id="control-of-fire"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/control-of-fire">Control of fire</a></dt><dd>The control of fire by early humans gave warmth, light, protection from predators and a means of cooking; the earliest known controlled use of fire took place in Wonderwerk Cave, South Africa, 1.79–1.0 million years ago.</dd></div><div id="copper"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a></dt><dd>Copper is a reddish metal used since ancient times for tools, ornaments and, alloyed with tin, bronze.</dd></div><div id="copper-ingot"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/copper-ingot">Copper ingot</a> <span class="gl-aka">(copper ingots)</span></dt><dd>Copper ingots are cast bars or shapes of copper used for trade and storage of value.</dd></div><div id="copper-smelting"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/copper-smelting">Copper smelting</a></dt><dd>Copper smelting is the production of copper from its ores at high temperature; the world's oldest securely dated evidence for it, from c. 5000 BCE, was found at Belovode on Rudnik mountain in Serbia.</dd></div><div id="coptic-pope-of-alexandria"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/coptic-pope-of-alexandria">Coptic Pope of Alexandria</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Coptic Patriarch of Alexandria)</span></dt><dd>The Coptic Pope of Alexandria is the patriarch and head of the Coptic Orthodox Church, the Miaphysite church of Egypt that separated from the Chalcedonian church after 451.</dd></div><div id="corvee-labour"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">Corvée labour</a> <span class="gl-aka">(corvée)</span></dt><dd>Corvée labour is unpaid work required by a state from its subjects, often for building and maintaining public works or for military service.</dd></div><div id="corvus"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/corvus">Corvus</a></dt><dd>The corvus was a boarding bridge with a spike at its end, used by the Roman navy during the First Punic War.</dd></div><div id="cotton"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cotton">Cotton</a></dt><dd>Cotton is a plant fibre spun and woven into textiles, one of the most important traded commodities in history.</dd></div><div id="cotton-gin"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cotton-gin">Cotton gin</a></dt><dd>The cotton gin is a machine that separates cotton fibres from their seeds; the earliest evidence of it is a single-roller gin depicted in 5th-century Buddhist paintings in the Ajanta Caves in western India.</dd></div><div id="counterweight-trebuchet"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/counterweight-trebuchet">Counterweight trebuchet</a> <span class="gl-aka">(counterweight trebuchets)</span></dt><dd>A counterweight trebuchet is a siege engine that uses a heavy falling weight to swing a throwing arm, capable of hurling large stones over long distances.</dd></div><div id="cowrie-shell"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">Cowrie shell</a> <span class="gl-aka">(cowrie)</span></dt><dd>The cowrie shell is the shell of a small sea snail, used as currency across Africa and Asia.</dd></div><div id="coyotlatelco"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/coyotlatelco">Coyotlatelco</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Coyotlatelco culture)</span></dt><dd>Coyotlatelco is a style of red-on-buff pottery, and by extension the archaeological culture associated with it, found in the Basin of Mexico in the centuries after the decline of Teotihuacan, roughly 600 to 900 CE.</dd></div><div id="cross-of-lorraine"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cross-of-lorraine">Cross of Lorraine</a></dt><dd>The Cross of Lorraine is a cross with two horizontal bars, originally the Cross of Anjou, which René II brought into use as an emblem of the dukes of Lorraine in the later fifteenth century.</dd></div><div id="crossbow"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">Crossbow</a></dt><dd>A crossbow is a bow mounted horizontally on a stock, shot by a trigger mechanism, allowing troops with limited training to shoot powerful bolts.</dd></div><div id="crown-dependencies"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/crown-dependencies">Crown Dependencies</a></dt><dd>The Crown Dependencies are the Bailiwick of Jersey, the Bailiwick of Guernsey and the Isle of Man, self-governing possessions of the British Crown.</dd></div><div id="crucible-steel"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/crucible-steel">Crucible steel</a></dt><dd>Crucible steel is steel made by melting iron, often with fluxes, in a crucible, which allows precise control of its carbon content; it was first developed in southern India and Sri Lanka by the wootz process, with material as early as 300…</dd></div><div id="crucifixion"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/crucifixion">Crucifixion</a> <span class="gl-aka">(crucify)</span></dt><dd>Crucifixion is a method of execution in which the condemned is fastened to a cross or stake and left to die.</dd></div><div id="cumbric"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cumbric">Cumbric</a></dt><dd>Cumbric was the Brittonic Celtic language of the early medieval north of Britain, spoken in the kingdom of Strathclyde and in Cumbria.</dd></div><div id="cuneiform"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">Cuneiform</a></dt><dd>Cuneiform is a writing system of wedge-shaped signs pressed into clay tablets, invented in Mesopotamia in the late fourth millennium BCE.</dd></div><div id="cupbearer"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cupbearer">Cupbearer</a></dt><dd>A cupbearer was a court official who served drinks to the king, a position of trust and access to the ruler.</dd></div><div id="customs-union"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">Customs union</a></dt><dd>A customs union is an agreement among territories to remove tariffs between them and adopt a common external tariff.</dd></div><div id="cyfraith-hywel"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cyfraith-hywel">Cyfraith Hywel</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Laws of Hywel Dda)</span></dt><dd>Cyfraith Hywel, the law of Hywel, was the native law of medieval Wales, traditionally said to have been codified under Hywel Dda in the 10th century.</dd></div><div id="cypro-minoan-syllabary"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cypro-minoan-syllabary">Cypro-Minoan syllabary</a></dt><dd>The Cypro-Minoan syllabary is an undeciphered script used on Cyprus in the Late Bronze Age, from about 1550 to 1050 BCE.</dd></div><div id="cyzicene"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/cyzicene">Cyzicene</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Cyzicene stater)</span></dt><dd>Cyzicenes were electrum coins struck by the Greek city of Cyzicus on the Sea of Marmara from the 6th to the 4th century BCE.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-d"><h2>D</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="damel"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/damel">Damel</a></dt><dd>Damel was the title of the kings of Cayor, a Wolof kingdom on the coast of Senegal.</dd></div><div id="danegeld"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/danegeld">Danegeld</a></dt><dd>Danegeld was a tax raised in Anglo-Saxon England to pay tribute to Viking raiders, above all from 991 under Æthelred the Unready, when large sums were paid to Danish armies to stop their attacks.</dd></div><div id="darb-el-arbain"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/darb-el-arbain">Darb el-Arba'in</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Darb El Arba'in)</span></dt><dd>Darb el-Arba'in, the 'forty-day road', was a caravan route linking Darfur to Asyut in Upper Egypt.</dd></div><div id="daric"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/daric">Daric</a></dt><dd>The daric was a gold coin of the Achaemenid Empire, introduced under Darius I.</dd></div><div id="dauphin-of-france"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/dauphin-of-france">Dauphin of France</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Dauphin of Viennois)</span></dt><dd>Dauphin of France was the title of the heir apparent to the French throne from 1350 to 1830.</dd></div><div id="decimal-military-organisation"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/decimal-military-organisation">Decimal military organisation</a> <span class="gl-aka">(decimal military system, decimal organisation)</span></dt><dd>Decimal military organisation is a system of dividing an army into units of ten, a hundred, a thousand and ten thousand, each under its own commander.</dd></div><div id="deflation"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/deflation">Deflation</a> <span class="gl-aka">(deflationary)</span></dt><dd>Deflation is a sustained fall in the general level of prices, often linked to a shortage of money.</dd></div><div id="deforestation"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/deforestation">Deforestation</a></dt><dd>Deforestation is the large-scale clearing of forests.</dd></div><div id="denarius"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/denarius">Denarius</a></dt><dd>The denarius was the standard silver coin of the Roman Empire for several centuries.</dd></div><div id="deportation"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/deportation">Deportation</a> <span class="gl-aka">(mass deportation)</span></dt><dd>Deportation refers to the forced removal of people from their homes to other places.</dd></div><div id="deutsche-mark"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/deutsche-mark">Deutsche Mark</a></dt><dd>The Deutsche Mark was the currency of West Germany from June 1948 and of reunified Germany from 1990 until it was replaced by the euro in 2002.</dd></div><div id="devanagari"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/devanagari">Devanagari</a></dt><dd>Devanagari is an Indian script, written from left to right with a characteristic horizontal headline, in which each consonant carries an inherent vowel.</dd></div><div id="dharmachakra"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/dharmachakra">Dharmachakra</a></dt><dd>The Dharmachakra, or Wheel of the Law, is a Buddhist symbol representing the teaching of the Buddha, recalling his first sermon at Sarnath, known as setting the wheel of the Dharma in motion.</dd></div><div id="dhu-raydan"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/dhu-raydan">Dhu Raydan</a></dt><dd>Dhu Raydan, after Raydan, the royal palace at the Himyarite capital Zafar, was the name by which the Himyarite kingdom of south Arabia appears in its inscriptions.</dd></div><div id="dinar"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/dinar">Dinar</a></dt><dd>The dinar was the gold coin of the Islamic world, first issued in the Umayyad period.</dd></div><div id="dirham"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/dirham">Dirham</a> <span class="gl-aka">(dirhams)</span></dt><dd>The dirham was the silver coin of the Islamic world.</dd></div><div id="distillation"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/distillation">Distillation</a></dt><dd>Distillation is the separation of the substances in a liquid mixture by selective boiling and the condensation of the vapours in a still; Akkadian tablets of c. 1200 BCE describing perfumery show that a primitive form was known to the…</dd></div><div id="dome"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/dome">Dome</a></dt><dd>A dome is an architectural element resembling the hollow upper half of a sphere; the earliest found may be four dwellings of mammoth tusks and bones at Mezhirich in Ukraine, dated between 19,280 and 11,700 BCE.</dd></div><div id="domestication"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/domestication">Domestication</a></dt><dd>Domestication is a long-term relationship in which one species takes over the control and care of another to obtain resources such as meat, milk or labour; domestication by humans began with the dog at least 15,000 years ago.</dd></div><div id="dongmaeng"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/dongmaeng">Dongmaeng</a></dt><dd>Dongmaeng was a great state festival of Goguryeo held in the tenth month of the year to give thanks to Heaven, recorded in the Chinese Records of the Three Kingdoms.</dd></div><div id="druk-desi"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/druk-desi">Druk Desi</a></dt><dd>The Druk Desi was the civil ruler of Bhutan under the dual system of government created by Ngawang Namgyal, the Zhabdrung, in the seventeenth century, while the Je Khenpo headed the monastic body.</dd></div><div id="druk-gyalpo"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/druk-gyalpo">Druk Gyalpo</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Dragon King)</span></dt><dd>Druk Gyalpo, 'Dragon King', is the title of the head of state of Bhutan.</dd></div><div id="dry-stone-construction"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/dry-stone-construction">Dry-stone construction</a> <span class="gl-aka">(dry-stone)</span></dt><dd>Dry-stone construction is a building technique in which walls are made from carefully fitted stones without mortar.</dd></div><div id="duke-of-atholl"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-atholl">Duke of Atholl</a></dt><dd>Duke of Atholl is a title in the peerage of Scotland created in 1703 for John Murray, Marquess of Atholl.</dd></div><div id="duke-of-aveiro"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-aveiro">Duke of Aveiro</a> <span class="gl-aka">(House of Aveiro)</span></dt><dd>Duke of Aveiro was a Portuguese ducal title created in 1535 for João de Lencastre, a grandson of King John II.</dd></div><div id="dynamite"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/dynamite">Dynamite</a></dt><dd>Dynamite is an explosive made of nitroglycerin, an absorbent such as clay or diatomaceous earth, and stabilisers, invented in 1866 by the Swedish chemist Alfred Nobel, who stabilised nitroglycerin with earth from the Elbe near his factory…</dd></div><div id="dzud"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/dzud">Dzud</a></dt><dd>Dzud is a Mongolian term for a catastrophic winter, when heavy snow, ice or extreme cold prevents livestock from grazing and causes mass deaths of animals.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-e"><h2>E</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="earl-of-clanricarde"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-clanricarde">Earl of Clanricarde</a></dt><dd>Earl of Clanricarde is a title in the Peerage of Ireland created in 1543 for Ulick na gCeann Burke, chief of the Burkes of south County Galway, under Henry VIII's policy of surrender and regrant.</dd></div><div id="earl-of-derby"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-derby">Earl of Derby</a></dt><dd>Earl of Derby is a title in the peerage of England held since 1485 by the Stanley family.</dd></div><div id="earl-of-kildare"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-kildare">Earl of Kildare</a></dt><dd>Earl of Kildare is a title in the peerage of Ireland created in 1316 for John FitzGerald and held by the Geraldine (FitzGerald) family.</dd></div><div id="earl-of-mercia"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-mercia">Earl of Mercia</a></dt><dd>The Earl of Mercia was one of the great territorial earls of late Anglo-Saxon England, governing much of the old Mercian kingdom under the kings of England.</dd></div><div id="early-bronze-age"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a></dt><dd>The Early Bronze Age is the first phase of the Bronze Age in the Near East and the Aegean, roughly 3300 to 2000 BCE.</dd></div><div id="early-classic-period"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/early-classic-period">Early Classic period</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Early Classic Maya)</span></dt><dd>The Early Classic period is the first part of the Classic era of Maya civilisation, from about 250 to 600 CE.</dd></div><div id="early-minoan-period"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/early-minoan-period">Early Minoan period</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Early Minoan)</span></dt><dd>The Early Minoan period is the first phase of the Bronze Age Minoan civilisation of Crete, from about 3100 to 2100 BCE.</dd></div><div id="east-semitic-languages"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/east-semitic-languages">East Semitic languages</a></dt><dd>The East Semitic languages are an extinct branch of the Semitic language family, spoken in ancient Mesopotamia and Syria.</dd></div><div id="ebony"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ebony">Ebony</a></dt><dd>Ebony is a dense, dark hardwood from tropical Africa that was highly valued as a luxury material in the ancient world.</dd></div><div id="ecological-warfare"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ecological-warfare">Ecological warfare</a></dt><dd>Ecological warfare refers to military methods that target an enemy's environment and resources, such as water supplies or farmland.</dd></div><div id="egyptian-hieroglyphs"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/egyptian-hieroglyphs">Egyptian hieroglyphs</a> <span class="gl-aka">(hieroglyphics)</span></dt><dd>Egyptian hieroglyphs were the formal writing system of ancient Egypt, using pictorial signs for sounds and words.</dd></div><div id="eid-al-ghadir"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/eid-al-ghadir">Eid al-Ghadir</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Feast of Ghadir)</span></dt><dd>Eid al-Ghadir is a Shia Muslim festival held on 18 Dhu al-Hijja to commemorate the Prophet Muhammad's sermon at Ghadir Khumm in 632, in which Shia Muslims see the designation of Ali ibn Abi Talib as his successor.</dd></div><div id="el-nino"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/el-nino">El Niño</a> <span class="gl-aka">(El Niño–Southern Oscillation)</span></dt><dd>El Niño is the periodic warming of surface waters in the central and eastern tropical Pacific, part of a climate cycle that recurs every few years.</dd></div><div id="elamite-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/elamite-language">Elamite language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Elamite administrative language)</span></dt><dd>Elamite is the extinct language of ancient Elam, written first in its own scripts and later in cuneiform.</dd></div><div id="elector-palatine"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Elector of the Palatinate)</span></dt><dd>Elector Palatine was the title of the Count Palatine of the Rhine as one of the prince-electors of the Holy Roman Empire, confirmed by the Golden Bull of 1356.</dd></div><div id="electric-generator"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/electric-generator">Electric generator</a></dt><dd>An electric generator is a machine that converts mechanical energy into electrical energy; the first electromagnetic generator, the Faraday disk, was built by the British scientist Michael Faraday in 1831, a copper disc turning between…</dd></div><div id="elevator"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/elevator">Elevator</a></dt><dd>An elevator, or lift, is a machine that carries people or goods vertically between levels; the earliest known reference, by the Roman architect Vitruvius, reports that Archimedes built one, probably in 236 BCE.</dd></div><div id="elmina-note"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/elmina-note">Elmina Note</a></dt><dd>The Elmina Note was a document entitling an African power to a regular rent paid by the Dutch for the land of their castle at Elmina on the Gold Coast.</dd></div><div id="english-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/english-language">English language</a></dt><dd>English is a Germanic language that originated in England.</dd></div><div id="englishry"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/englishry">Englishry</a></dt><dd>The Englishry was the lowland part of a marcher lordship in Wales, settled by English and Flemish colonists and run under English law and manorial custom, as distinct from the upland Welshry, where Welsh tenants kept Welsh law and customs.</dd></div><div id="entrepot"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">Entrepôt</a> <span class="gl-aka">(entrepot)</span></dt><dd>An entrepôt is a port or trading centre where goods are imported, stored and re-exported, often without further processing.</dd></div><div id="erligang-culture"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/erligang-culture">Erligang culture</a></dt><dd>The Erligang culture is a Bronze Age culture of China dated to about 1600–1400 BCE, named after a site at Zhengzhou in Henan.</dd></div><div id="esperanto"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/esperanto">Esperanto</a></dt><dd>Esperanto is a constructed international language created by the Polish ophthalmologist L. L. Zamenhof and first published in 1887.</dd></div><div id="eteocypriot-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/eteocypriot-language">Eteocypriot language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Eteocypriot)</span></dt><dd>Eteocypriot is the name given to a non-Greek language of ancient Cyprus, known from inscriptions written in the Cypriot syllabary between about the 8th and 4th centuries BCE.</dd></div><div id="etsu-nupe"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/etsu-nupe">Etsu Nupe</a></dt><dd>Etsu Nupe is the title of the traditional ruler of the Nupe people of central Nigeria.</dd></div><div id="eunuch"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">Eunuch</a></dt><dd>A eunuch is a castrated man, and in many courts eunuchs served as palace officials, guards of the royal household and administrators.</dd></div><div id="eurobond"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/eurobond">Eurobond</a></dt><dd>A Eurobond is an international bond issued in a currency other than that of the country where it is sold, usually underwritten by an international syndicate of banks.</dd></div><div id="eyeglasses"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/eyeglasses">Eyeglasses</a></dt><dd>Eyeglasses, or spectacles, are lenses held in a frame in front of the eyes; the first are estimated to have been made in central Italy, most likely in Pisa or Florence, by about 1290.</dd></div><div id="eze-nri"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/eze-nri">Eze Nri</a></dt><dd>Eze Nri was the title of the priest-king of Nri, an Igbo sacred kingdom in south-eastern Nigeria.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-f"><h2>F</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="fatwa"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">Fatwa</a></dt><dd>A fatwa is a formal legal opinion issued by a qualified Islamic jurist.</dd></div><div id="federalism"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/federalism">Federalism</a> <span class="gl-aka">(federalisation, federal autonomy)</span></dt><dd>Federalism is a system of government in which power is divided between a central authority and constituent units.</dd></div><div id="feigned-retreat"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">Feigned retreat</a></dt><dd>The feigned retreat is a tactic in which an army pretends to flee in order to draw the enemy into breaking formation, then turns to attack.</dd></div><div id="feudalism"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/feudalism">Feudalism</a> <span class="gl-aka">(semifedal, feudal)</span></dt><dd>Feudalism is a term for systems in which land was granted in return for military service and loyalty, creating hierarchies of lords and vassals.</dd></div><div id="field-artillery"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">Field artillery</a> <span class="gl-aka">(cannon)</span></dt><dd>Field artillery refers to mobile cannon used on the battlefield.</dd></div><div id="fire-arrow"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/fire-arrow">Fire arrow</a> <span class="gl-aka">(fire arrows)</span></dt><dd>A fire arrow was an arrow carrying an incendiary or gunpowder charge.</dd></div><div id="fire-lance"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/fire-lance">Fire lance</a> <span class="gl-aka">(huoqiang)</span></dt><dd>The fire lance was an early gunpowder weapon: a tube of gunpowder, usually bamboo, strapped to a spear or other polearm, with the earliest evidence from China in 950.</dd></div><div id="first-intermediate-period"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a></dt><dd>The First Intermediate Period is the era of ancient Egyptian history between the collapse of the Old Kingdom and the reunification of the country in the Middle Kingdom.</dd></div><div id="first-sea-lord"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/first-sea-lord">First Sea Lord</a></dt><dd>The First Sea Lord is the professional head of the British Royal Navy and the senior naval officer on the Admiralty Board.</dd></div><div id="five-year-plans-of-the-soviet-union"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/five-year-plans-of-the-soviet-union">Five-year plans of the Soviet Union</a></dt><dd>The five-year plans were the centralised economic plans by which the Soviet state directed production from 1928 until 1991.</dd></div><div id="flag-of-denmark"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/flag-of-denmark">Flag of Denmark</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Dannebrog)</span></dt><dd>The flag of Denmark, the Dannebrog, a white Scandinavian cross on a red field, is often said to be the oldest national flag in continuous use.</dd></div><div id="flax"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/flax">Flax</a></dt><dd>Flax is a plant grown for its fibres, which are made into linen, and for its seeds.</dd></div><div id="florentine-catasto"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/florentine-catasto">Florentine Catasto</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Catasto of 1427)</span></dt><dd>The Florentine Catasto of 1427 was a tax survey introduced by the Republic of Florence, which required every household in the city and its territory to declare its members, property, investments and debts.</dd></div><div id="fon-title"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/fon-title">Fon (title)</a></dt><dd>Fon is the title of the sacred kings of the chiefdoms and kingdoms of the Cameroon Grassfields, such as Nso, Bafut and Bali-Nyonga.</dd></div><div id="foreign-orientals"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/foreign-orientals">Foreign Orientals</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Vreemde Oosterlingen)</span></dt><dd>Foreign Orientals (Vreemde Oosterlingen) was a legal category in the Dutch East Indies for residents of Asian origin who were neither Europeans nor natives, chiefly Chinese, Arabs and Indians.</dd></div><div id="fortified-camp"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/fortified-camp">Fortified camp</a> <span class="gl-aka">(fortified camps)</span></dt><dd>A fortified camp is a defended encampment built by an army on campaign.</dd></div><div id="four-asian-tigers"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/four-asian-tigers">Four Asian Tigers</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Four Little Dragons)</span></dt><dd>The Four Asian Tigers are Hong Kong, Singapore, South Korea and Taiwan, economies that industrialised rapidly from the 1960s to the 1990s.</dd></div><div id="frankokratia"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/frankokratia">Frankokratia</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Latinokratia)</span></dt><dd>Frankokratia ('rule of the Franks') is the period of Western, chiefly French and Italian, rule in Greek lands after the Fourth Crusade of 1204.</dd></div><div id="french-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/french-language">French language</a></dt><dd>French is a Romance language that developed from Latin in France.</dd></div><div id="fuhrer"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/fuhrer">Führer</a></dt><dd>Führer, German for 'leader', was the title used by Adolf Hitler as head of the Nazi Party and, from August 1934, as head of the German state after he merged the offices of president and chancellor on the death of Paul von Hindenburg.</dd></div><div id="fundus"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/fundus">Fundus</a></dt><dd>A fundus was a Roman landed estate or farm.</dd></div><div id="fur-trade"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">Fur trade</a> <span class="gl-aka">(furs)</span></dt><dd>The fur trade was the commerce in animal pelts, highly valued for clothing and as luxury goods.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-g"><h2>G</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="gabunga"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/gabunga">Gabunga</a></dt><dd>Gabunga was the title of the admiral of the Kingdom of Buganda's fleet of war canoes on Lake Victoria, a hereditary office held by the head of the Lungfish (Mamba) clan.</dd></div><div id="galician-portuguese"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/galician-portuguese">Galician-Portuguese</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Old Galician)</span></dt><dd>Galician-Portuguese was the Romance language of medieval Galicia and northern Portugal, from which modern Galician and Portuguese developed.</dd></div><div id="galilean-moons"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/galilean-moons">Galilean moons</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Medicean Stars)</span></dt><dd>The Galilean moons are the four largest moons of Jupiter, Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto, discovered by Galileo Galilei with his telescope in January 1610.</dd></div><div id="galleon"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/galleon">Galleon</a></dt><dd>A galleon was a large, multi-decked sailing ship used from the sixteenth century for war and long-distance trade.</dd></div><div id="galley"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/galley">Galley</a> <span class="gl-aka">(galleys)</span></dt><dd>A galley is a warship propelled mainly by oars, dominant in Mediterranean warfare for centuries.</dd></div><div id="gallo-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/gallo-language">Gallo language</a></dt><dd>Gallo is a Romance language of the langue d'oïl group, spoken in eastern Brittany around Rennes, Saint-Malo and Nantes.</dd></div><div id="gamay"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/gamay">Gamay</a></dt><dd>Gamay is a red wine grape of eastern France, the main grape of Beaujolais.</dd></div><div id="gandhari-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/gandhari-language">Gandhari language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Gandhari Prakrit)</span></dt><dd>Gandhari is a Middle Indo-Aryan language, or Prakrit, of the ancient region of Gandhara in what is now north-western Pakistan and eastern Afghanistan.</dd></div><div id="ganjifa"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ganjifa">Ganjifa</a></dt><dd>Ganjifa is a family of card games played with round, hand-painted cards, introduced to India from Persia and popular at the Mughal court in the 16th century.</dd></div><div id="garhjat-states"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/garhjat-states">Garhjat states</a></dt><dd>The Garhjat states were the small princely states of the hill and forest country of Odisha, whose name refers to the forts, garh, from which their Rajas ruled.</dd></div><div id="gatling-gun"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/gatling-gun">Gatling gun</a></dt><dd>The Gatling gun is a hand-cranked, rapid-firing gun with several rotating barrels, patented by the American inventor Richard Gatling in 1862.</dd></div><div id="gauleiter"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/gauleiter">Gauleiter</a></dt><dd>A Gauleiter was the Nazi Party's leader of a regional branch, or Gau, in Germany and later in annexed territories.</dd></div><div id="gbe-languages"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/gbe-languages">Gbe languages</a></dt><dd>The Gbe languages are a group of closely related languages of the Volta–Niger family spoken from south-eastern Ghana through Togo and Benin to south-western Nigeria.</dd></div><div id="geez"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/geez">Ge'ez</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Ge'ez script)</span></dt><dd>Ge'ez is an ancient Semitic language of Ethiopia and Eritrea, with its own script.</dd></div><div id="general-anaesthesia"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/general-anaesthesia">General anaesthesia</a> <span class="gl-aka">(general anesthesia)</span></dt><dd>General anaesthesia is a drug-induced loss of consciousness from which a patient cannot be roused even by pain; the first successful surgery on record under it was performed by the Japanese surgeon Hanaoka Seishū on 14 November 1804.</dd></div><div id="genocide"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/genocide">Genocide</a></dt><dd>Genocide is the deliberate destruction of a national, ethnic, racial or religious group.</dd></div><div id="german-emperor"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Deutscher Kaiser)</span></dt><dd>German Emperor was the title of the head of the German Empire from 1871 to 1918, always held by the King of Prussia.</dd></div><div id="germanisation"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/germanisation">Germanisation</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Germanization)</span></dt><dd>Germanisation is the spread of the German language, people and culture into other lands, through settlement or state policy.</dd></div><div id="gilchrist-thomas-process"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/gilchrist-thomas-process">Gilchrist–Thomas process</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Thomas process, basic Bessemer process)</span></dt><dd>The Gilchrist–Thomas process is a method of making steel from phosphorus-rich iron ore, developed by the British cousins Sidney Gilchrist Thomas and Percy Gilchrist and made public in 1879.</dd></div><div id="glassblowing"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/glassblowing">Glassblowing</a> <span class="gl-aka">(glass blowing)</span></dt><dd>Glassblowing is the technique of inflating molten glass into a bubble with a blowpipe and shaping it, developed by Phoenician and Syrian craftsmen in the 1st century BCE, in what are now Lebanon and Israel.</dd></div><div id="glassmaking"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/glassmaking">Glassmaking</a></dt><dd>Glassmaking is the craft of producing glass objects.</dd></div><div id="gods-wife-of-amun"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/gods-wife-of-amun">God's Wife of Amun</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Divine Adoratrice of Amun)</span></dt><dd>God's Wife of Amun was the title of the highest-ranking priestess of the god Amun at Thebes.</dd></div><div id="gold-trade"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">Gold trade</a> <span class="gl-aka">(gold-salt, trans-Saharan gold)</span></dt><dd>The gold trade refers to the commerce in West African gold, often exchanged for Saharan salt and carried across the desert to North Africa and the Mediterranean.</dd></div><div id="golden-urn"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/golden-urn">Golden Urn</a></dt><dd>The Golden Urn was a method introduced by the Qing Qianlong Emperor in 1793 for choosing high Tibetan and Mongolian reincarnate lamas, including the Dalai and Panchen Lamas.</dd></div><div id="gonfaloniere-of-justice"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/gonfaloniere-of-justice">Gonfaloniere of Justice</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Gonfaloniere di Giustizia)</span></dt><dd>The Gonfaloniere of Justice was the highest office of the Republic of Florence, created by the Ordinances of Justice of 1293 to command a militia enforcing the laws against the magnates.</dd></div><div id="gorani-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/gorani-language">Gorani language</a></dt><dd>Gorani is a Northwestern Iranian language spoken in parts of the Zagros on both sides of the Iran–Iraq border, closely related to Zazaki.</dd></div><div id="gosanke"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/gosanke">Gosanke</a></dt><dd>The Gosanke, the 'three honourable houses', were the three senior branch families of the Tokugawa, founded by sons of Tokugawa Ieyasu: the Owari, Kii and Mito houses.</dd></div><div id="grand-duke-of-tuscany"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a></dt><dd>Grand Duke of Tuscany was the title created by Pope Pius V in 1569 for Cosimo I de' Medici, ruler of Florence and Siena, and confirmed by the emperor in 1576.</dd></div><div id="grand-pensionary"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/grand-pensionary">Grand Pensionary</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Raadpensionaris)</span></dt><dd>The Grand Pensionary was the leading official of the province of Holland in the Dutch Republic, its chief legal adviser and spokesman in the States General, and often the effective director of the Republic's policy.</dd></div><div id="grand-zupan"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/grand-zupan">Grand Župan</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Veliki župan)</span></dt><dd>Grand Župan was the title of the supreme ruler of several early medieval South Slavic states, above the župans who governed individual districts.</dd></div><div id="grantha-script"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/grantha-script">Grantha script</a></dt><dd>The Grantha script is a South Indian script derived from Brahmi, used from Pallava times to write Sanskrit in the Tamil country, where the Tamil script lacked letters for many Sanskrit sounds.</dd></div><div id="great-chief-of-the-ma"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/great-chief-of-the-ma">Great Chief of the Ma</a></dt><dd>Great Chief of the Ma was the title of the leaders of the Meshwesh, Libyan settlers in Egypt, during the Third Intermediate Period.</dd></div><div id="great-sun"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/great-sun">Great Sun</a></dt><dd>The Great Sun was the title of the paramount ruler of the Natchez people of the lower Mississippi valley.</dd></div><div id="greek-alphabet"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/greek-alphabet">Greek alphabet</a></dt><dd>The Greek alphabet is the script of the Greek language, in use since the late 9th or early 8th century BCE (conventionally about 800 BCE), with early samples from at least 775 BCE.</dd></div><div id="greek-fire"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/greek-fire">Greek fire</a></dt><dd>Greek fire was an incendiary weapon used by the Byzantine Empire, typically projected from siphons on warships, and able to burn on water.</dd></div><div id="greek-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a></dt><dd>The Greek language is an Indo-European language with a written history stretching back over three thousand years.</dd></div><div id="green-corn-ceremony"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/green-corn-ceremony">Green Corn Ceremony</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Busk)</span></dt><dd>The Green Corn Ceremony is the most important annual festival of the Muscogee (Creek), Seminole and other peoples of the south-eastern woodlands of North America.</dd></div><div id="grenzer"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/grenzer">Grenzer</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Grenzers, Military Frontiersmen)</span></dt><dd>Grenzer were the soldier-settlers of the Habsburg Military Frontier, the border zone against the Ottoman Empire in Croatia, Slavonia, Hungary and later Transylvania.</dd></div><div id="gross-domestic-product"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">Gross domestic product</a> <span class="gl-aka">(GDP, GNP)</span></dt><dd>Gross domestic product (GDP) is a measure of the total value of goods and services produced within an economy over a given period.</dd></div><div id="groundnut"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/groundnut">Groundnut</a> <span class="gl-aka">(groundnuts)</span></dt><dd>The groundnut, or peanut, is a legume crop originally from South America that became widely cultivated in West Africa.</dd></div><div id="groundwater-depletion"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">Groundwater depletion</a> <span class="gl-aka">(resource depletion, groundwater)</span></dt><dd>Groundwater depletion is the exhaustion of underground water reserves when extraction exceeds natural recharge.</dd></div><div id="guarani-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/guarani-language">Guarani language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Paraguayan Guarani)</span></dt><dd>Guarani is a Tupi–Guarani language of South America and one of the two official languages of Paraguay, alongside Spanish, spoken by most Paraguayans regardless of background.</dd></div><div id="guerrilla-warfare"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">Guerrilla warfare</a> <span class="gl-aka">(guerrilla)</span></dt><dd>Guerrilla warfare is a form of irregular warfare in which small, mobile forces use raids, ambushes and knowledge of terrain against larger conventional armies.</dd></div><div id="guild"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/guild">Guild</a></dt><dd>A guild is an association of craftsmen or merchants practising the same trade, organised to regulate production, training and standards and to protect members' interests.</dd></div><div id="gunpowder"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">Gunpowder</a></dt><dd>Gunpowder is an explosive mixture of saltpetre, charcoal and sulphur, first developed in China.</dd></div><div id="guregen"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/guregen">Güregen</a></dt><dd>Güregen, meaning 'royal son-in-law', was a title borne in the Mongol world by men who married into the family of Genghis Khan.</dd></div><div id="gutsherrschaft"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/gutsherrschaft">Gutsherrschaft</a></dt><dd>Gutsherrschaft is the German term for the manorial system that developed east of the Elbe from the 15th and 16th centuries.</dd></div><div id="gymnasium"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">Gymnasium</a></dt><dd>A gymnasium was a Greek institution for physical training and education, which also served as a centre of social and intellectual life.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-h"><h2>H</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="haber-process"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/haber-process">Haber process</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Haber–Bosch process)</span></dt><dd>The Haber process, or Haber–Bosch process, is the main industrial method of making ammonia, combining nitrogen from the air with hydrogen over an iron catalyst; the German chemist Fritz Haber demonstrated it in the laboratory in 1909, and…</dd></div><div id="hadiyya-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/hadiyya-language">Hadiyya language</a></dt><dd>Hadiyya is a Highland East Cushitic language spoken by the Hadiya people of southern Ethiopia, south-west of Addis Ababa, by more than a million people.</dd></div><div id="hadramautic-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/hadramautic-language">Hadramautic language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Hadramitic)</span></dt><dd>Hadramautic was one of the Ancient South Arabian languages, written in the Kingdom of Hadramawt in what is now eastern Yemen from the early first millennium BCE until about the 4th century CE.</dd></div><div id="haiku"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/haiku">Haiku</a></dt><dd>Haiku is a short form of Japanese poetry, traditionally of three phrases following a five-seven-five syllable pattern and often evoking nature and the seasons.</dd></div><div id="haitian-creole"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/haitian-creole">Haitian Creole</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Kreyòl)</span></dt><dd>Haitian Creole is a French-based creole language spoken by almost the whole population of Haiti.</dd></div><div id="hajj"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a></dt><dd>The hajj is the pilgrimage to Mecca that every Muslim who is able is expected to make at least once in a lifetime, one of the five pillars of Islam.</dd></div><div id="hallstatt-culture"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Hallstatt period)</span></dt><dd>The Hallstatt culture was the main archaeological culture of western and central Europe in the early Iron Age, from about 800 to 450 BCE, and is commonly associated with speakers of Proto-Celtic.</dd></div><div id="hand-cannon"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/hand-cannon">Hand cannon</a> <span class="gl-aka">(hand cannons)</span></dt><dd>The hand cannon was an early firearm consisting of a simple metal tube fired by igniting gunpowder by hand.</dd></div><div id="hangul"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/hangul">Hangul</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Hangeul, Korean alphabet)</span></dt><dd>Hangul is the Korean alphabet, created in 1443 under King Sejong of Joseon and promulgated in 1446 as the Hunminjeongeum, &quot;the proper sounds for instructing the people&quot;.</dd></div><div id="hanyu-pinyin"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/hanyu-pinyin">Hanyu Pinyin</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Pinyin)</span></dt><dd>Hanyu Pinyin is the official system for writing Standard Mandarin Chinese in the Latin alphabet, adopted by the People's Republic of China in 1958 and developed by a committee that included the linguist Zhou Youguang.</dd></div><div id="hassaniya-arabic"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/hassaniya-arabic">Hassaniya Arabic</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Hassaniya)</span></dt><dd>Hassaniya Arabic is the variety of Arabic spoken in Mauritania, Western Sahara, southern Morocco and parts of Algeria, Mali and Senegal.</dd></div><div id="hattic-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/hattic-language">Hattic language</a></dt><dd>Hattic was the language of the Hattians of central Anatolia in the 3rd and early 2nd millennium BCE.</dd></div><div id="haveli"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/haveli">Haveli</a></dt><dd>A haveli is a traditional townhouse or mansion of the Indian subcontinent, built around one or more inner courtyards.</dd></div><div id="heavy-cavalry"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">Heavy cavalry</a></dt><dd>Heavy cavalry refers to mounted troops with substantial armour and weapons, used for shock action against enemy formations.</dd></div><div id="hebrew"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a></dt><dd>Hebrew is a Semitic language, the language of the Hebrew Bible and of ancient Israel.</dd></div><div id="hellanodikai"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/hellanodikai">Hellanodikai</a></dt><dd>The Hellanodikai, 'judges of the Greeks', were the officials who organised and judged the ancient Olympic Games.</dd></div><div id="heller-coin"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/heller-coin">Heller (coin)</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Haller pfennig)</span></dt><dd>The Heller was a German coin first struck at the imperial mint of Schwäbisch Hall in the 12th century as a silver pfennig, the Haller pfennig, from which its name comes.</dd></div><div id="high-king-of-ireland"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/high-king-of-ireland">High King of Ireland</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Ard Rí)</span></dt><dd>The High King of Ireland, Ard Rí in Irish, was the title claimed by kings who asserted supremacy over the island's many kingdoms.</dd></div><div id="highland-east-cushitic-languages"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/highland-east-cushitic-languages">Highland East Cushitic languages</a></dt><dd>The Highland East Cushitic languages are a branch of the Cushitic family spoken in the highlands of southern Ethiopia, in and around the Rift Valley.</dd></div><div id="hill-fort"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">Hill fort</a> <span class="gl-aka">(hill forts)</span></dt><dd>A hill fort is a fortification built on high ground, using the natural terrain for defence.</dd></div><div id="hindko"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/hindko">Hindko</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Hindko language)</span></dt><dd>Hindko is a group of Indo-Aryan dialects spoken in north-western Pakistan, especially in the Hazara region and the city of Peshawar in Khyber Pakhtunkhwa, and in parts of northern Punjab.</dd></div><div id="hindu-arabic-numeral-system"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/hindu-arabic-numeral-system">Hindu–Arabic numeral system</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Hindu-Arabic numerals)</span></dt><dd>The Hindu–Arabic numeral system is the decimal place-value system of writing numbers with ten digits, including zero, used throughout the world today.</dd></div><div id="hiri-motu"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/hiri-motu">Hiri Motu</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Police Motu)</span></dt><dd>Hiri Motu is a simplified form of the Motu language of the Port Moresby area and one of the national languages of Papua New Guinea.</dd></div><div id="hiv-aids"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/hiv-aids">HIV/AIDS</a></dt><dd>HIV/AIDS is the disease caused by the human immunodeficiency virus, which weakens the immune system and, untreated, leads to acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS).</dd></div><div id="hoplite"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">Hoplite</a></dt><dd>A hoplite was a heavily armed Greek infantryman, equipped with a large round shield, spear and armour, fighting in the close formation known as the phalanx.</dd></div><div id="horse-archer"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">Horse archer</a> <span class="gl-aka">(horse archers)</span></dt><dd>A horse archer is a mounted warrior who shoots with a bow from horseback, combining mobility with missile firepower.</dd></div><div id="horse-breeding"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/horse-breeding">Horse breeding</a> <span class="gl-aka">(horse-breeding)</span></dt><dd>Horse breeding is the raising of horses for transport, warfare and trade.</dd></div><div id="horse-collar"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/horse-collar">Horse collar</a></dt><dd>A horse collar is a padded harness collar that spreads a load across a horse's shoulders and chest without pressing on its windpipe; it was invented in China by the early medieval period.</dd></div><div id="horse-trade"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/horse-trade">Horse trade</a> <span class="gl-aka">(horses from North Africa)</span></dt><dd>The horse trade refers to the exchange of horses, often over long distances, for military and prestige purposes.</dd></div><div id="huerta"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/huerta">Huerta</a></dt><dd>A huerta is an irrigated agricultural area in Spain, especially around Valencia, Murcia and Granada, using irrigation channels developed in the Islamic period.</dd></div><div id="huizi"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/huizi">Huizi</a></dt><dd>Huizi was a form of paper money issued by the Southern Song government from the twelfth century.</dd></div><div id="huopao"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/huopao">Huopao</a></dt><dd>Huopao were incendiary bombs or fire projectiles used in Chinese warfare, often launched by trebuchets.</dd></div><div id="hwacha"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/hwacha">Hwacha</a> <span class="gl-aka">(hwach'a)</span></dt><dd>The hwacha was a Korean weapons platform, a wooden cart carrying a launch pad, first built in 1409 under the Joseon dynasty; it could fire up to 200 rocket-powered arrows, or volleys of arrows and bolts from gun barrels.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-i"><h2>I</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="inca-rope-bridge"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/inca-rope-bridge">Inca rope bridge</a></dt><dd>Inca rope bridges are simple suspension bridges of woven grass that the Inca Empire built over canyons, gorges and rivers as part of its road system.</dd></div><div id="incandescent-light-bulb"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/incandescent-light-bulb">Incandescent light bulb</a> <span class="gl-aka">(incandescent lamp, incandescent light globe)</span></dt><dd>An incandescent light bulb is an electric light in which current heats a filament until it glows inside an evacuated or gas-filled glass bulb; Humphry Davy made an electric incandescent light in 1802 at the Royal Institution of Great…</dd></div><div id="incense"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/incense">Incense</a></dt><dd>Incense is aromatic resin or plant material burned for its fragrance, especially in religious rituals.</dd></div><div id="indian-ocean-trade"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a></dt><dd>Indian Ocean trade refers to the maritime exchange of goods such as spices, textiles, gold, ivory and ceramics across the Indian Ocean, driven by seasonal monsoon winds.</dd></div><div id="indigo"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/indigo">Indigo</a></dt><dd>Indigo is a blue dye obtained from plants, widely used to colour textiles.</dd></div><div id="indo-european-languages"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/indo-european-languages">Indo-European languages</a></dt><dd>The Indo-European languages are a family of related languages spoken across Europe and much of western and southern Asia, descended from a reconstructed ancestor, Proto-Indo-European, probably spoken in the 4th millennium BCE.</dd></div><div id="indus-script"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/indus-script">Indus script</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Harappan script, Indus Valley script)</span></dt><dd>The Indus script, also called the Harappan script, is the set of signs used by the Indus Valley Civilisation, found chiefly on stamp seals, seal impressions and pottery.</dd></div><div id="inflation"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/inflation">Inflation</a></dt><dd>Inflation is a sustained rise in the general level of prices, reducing the purchasing power of money.</dd></div><div id="insulin"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/insulin">Insulin</a></dt><dd>Insulin is the hormone that regulates blood sugar, and its absence or failure causes diabetes.</dd></div><div id="integrated-circuit"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/integrated-circuit">Integrated circuit</a> <span class="gl-aka">(microchip)</span></dt><dd>An integrated circuit, or microchip, is an assembly of electronic components such as transistors, resistors and capacitors and their connections, made on a thin piece of semiconductor, usually silicon; Jack Kilby demonstrated the first…</dd></div><div id="intercession-of-the-theotokos"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/intercession-of-the-theotokos">Intercession of the Theotokos</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Pokrov)</span></dt><dd>The Intercession of the Theotokos (Pokrov) is an Eastern Orthodox feast, celebrated on 1 October (14 October in the Gregorian calendar), commemorating a vision of the Virgin Mary spreading her veil over the faithful in the Blachernae…</dd></div><div id="internal-combustion-engine"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/internal-combustion-engine">Internal combustion engine</a></dt><dd>An internal combustion engine is a heat engine in which fuel burns with air or another oxidiser inside the engine, and the expanding gases push directly on pistons, turbine blades, a rotor or a nozzle; Robert Street patented one in 1794.</dd></div><div id="iron-curtain"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/iron-curtain">Iron Curtain</a></dt><dd>The Iron Curtain was the political, military and physical boundary that divided Europe after the Second World War, separating the Soviet Union and the communist states of Central and Eastern Europe from the West.</dd></div><div id="iron-plough"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/iron-plough">Iron plough</a> <span class="gl-aka">(iron-tipped plough)</span></dt><dd>The iron plough, or iron-tipped plough, is a plough whose cutting edge is made of iron, allowing deeper and more efficient tillage.</dd></div><div id="iron-smelting"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/iron-smelting">Iron smelting</a> <span class="gl-aka">(iron production)</span></dt><dd>Iron smelting is the extraction of iron from ore by heating it in a furnace.</dd></div><div id="iron-working"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/iron-working">Iron working</a> <span class="gl-aka">(iron-working)</span></dt><dd>Iron working is the craft of smelting and forging iron to make tools and weapons.</dd></div><div id="ironclad"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ironclad">Ironclad</a> <span class="gl-aka">(ironclads)</span></dt><dd>An ironclad was a steam-powered warship protected by iron or steel armour plates, developed in the mid-nineteenth century.</dd></div><div id="iroquoian-languages"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/iroquoian-languages">Iroquoian languages</a></dt><dd>The Iroquoian languages are a family of Native American languages of eastern North America.</dd></div><div id="irrigation"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/irrigation">Irrigation</a></dt><dd>Irrigation is the practice of applying controlled amounts of water to land to help grow crops, with some of the earliest known use dating to the 6th millennium BCE in Khuzistan, south-west Iran.</dd></div><div id="itza-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/itza-language">Itza language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Itzaj)</span></dt><dd>Itza is a Mayan language of the Yucatecan branch, spoken by the Itza Maya of the Petén lakes in northern Guatemala.</dd></div><div id="ivory-trade"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">Ivory trade</a> <span class="gl-aka">(ivory)</span></dt><dd>The ivory trade is the commerce in elephant tusks, prized for carving and luxury goods.</dd></div><div id="iwan"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/iwan">Iwan</a> <span class="gl-aka">(four-iwan)</span></dt><dd>An iwan is a vaulted hall or space, walled on three sides and open on one, characteristic of Persian and Islamic architecture.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-j"><h2>J</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="jacquard-machine"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/jacquard-machine">Jacquard machine</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Jacquard loom)</span></dt><dd>The Jacquard machine is a device fitted to a loom that automates the weaving of complex patterns such as brocade and damask, controlled by a chain of punched cards; it was patented by Joseph Marie Jacquard in 1804.</dd></div><div id="japanese-tea-ceremony"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/japanese-tea-ceremony">Japanese tea ceremony</a> <span class="gl-aka">(tea ceremony)</span></dt><dd>The Japanese tea ceremony is a ritual preparation and serving of powdered green tea, shaped by Zen Buddhist ideals of simplicity and harmony.</dd></div><div id="jarl"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/jarl">Jarl</a></dt><dd>Jarl was a rank of the nobility in Scandinavia in the Viking Age and early Middle Ages: a chieftain appointed to rule a territory in a king's stead, or a sovereign prince.</dd></div><div id="jati"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/jati">Jati</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Jāti)</span></dt><dd>Jati is the term traditionally used in the Indian subcontinent for a cohesive group such as a caste, sub-caste, clan, tribe or religious sect, often translated approximately as &quot;caste&quot;.</dd></div><div id="javanese-calendar"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/javanese-calendar">Javanese calendar</a></dt><dd>The Javanese calendar was introduced by Sultan Agung of Mataram in 1633.</dd></div><div id="je-khenpo"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/je-khenpo">Je Khenpo</a></dt><dd>The Je Khenpo is the chief abbot of the Central Monastic Body of Bhutan and the country's highest religious authority.</dd></div><div id="jebtsundamba-khutughtu"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/jebtsundamba-khutughtu">Jebtsundamba Khutughtu</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Jebtsundamba Khutuktu)</span></dt><dd>The Jebtsundamba Khutughtu is the title of the highest reincarnate lama of Mongolian Buddhism, head of the Gelug school in Mongolia.</dd></div><div id="jemdet-nasr-period"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/jemdet-nasr-period">Jemdet Nasr period</a></dt><dd>The Jemdet Nasr period is an archaeological period of Mesopotamia, about 3100 to 2900 BCE, between the Uruk period and the Early Dynastic period.</dd></div><div id="jerriais"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/jerriais">Jèrriais</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Jersey French, Jersey Norman French)</span></dt><dd>Jèrriais is the Norman language of Jersey, a variety of Norman French spoken on the island since the Middle Ages.</dd></div><div id="jersey-royal"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/jersey-royal">Jersey Royal</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Jersey Royal potato)</span></dt><dd>The Jersey Royal is a variety of early new potato grown in Jersey, developed around 1880 by the farmer Hugh de la Haye from an unusually large potato with many eyes.</dd></div><div id="jet-engine"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/jet-engine">Jet engine</a></dt><dd>A jet engine is a reaction engine that discharges a fast-moving jet of heated gas to produce thrust; the first patent for using a gas turbine to power an aircraft was filed in 1921 by Maxime Guillaume, though his engine was never built.</dd></div><div id="jezzailchi"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/jezzailchi">Jezzailchi</a> <span class="gl-aka">(jezzailchis)</span></dt><dd>Jezzailchis were soldiers armed with the jezail, a long-barrelled musket used in Iran, Afghanistan and Central Asia.</dd></div><div id="jiaozi"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/jiaozi">Jiaozi</a></dt><dd>Jiaozi was a form of paper money that first appeared in Sichuan in the early eleventh century, initially issued by merchants and later taken over by the government.</dd></div><div id="jihad"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/jihad">Jihad</a></dt><dd>Jihad is an Islamic term meaning struggle or striving, which can refer to spiritual effort or to armed struggle in defence or expansion of the faith.</dd></div><div id="jong-ship"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/jong-ship">Jong (ship)</a></dt><dd>The jong was a large ocean-going ship of the Javanese and Malay world, capable of carrying large crews and cargoes.</dd></div><div id="jury-trial"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/jury-trial">Jury trial</a> <span class="gl-aka">(trial by jury)</span></dt><dd>Jury trial is a legal procedure in which a panel of citizens decides the facts of a case.</dd></div><div id="justicia-of-aragon"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/justicia-of-aragon">Justicia of Aragon</a></dt><dd>The Justicia of Aragon was a high judge of the medieval Kingdom of Aragon who arbitrated between the king and his subjects and could protect individuals against royal officials.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-k"><h2>K</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="kabaka-of-buganda"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka of Buganda</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Kabaka)</span></dt><dd>The Kabaka of Buganda is the title of the king of Buganda, the largest of the traditional kingdoms in present-day Uganda.</dd></div><div id="kabuki"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kabuki">Kabuki</a></dt><dd>Kabuki is a form of Japanese theatre combining drama, music and dance, known for stylised acting and elaborate costumes.</dd></div><div id="kalachuri-chedi-era"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kalachuri-chedi-era">Kalachuri-Chedi era</a></dt><dd>The Kalachuri-Chedi era is a calendar era of ancient India whose first year began in 248 or 249 CE.</dd></div><div id="kannada"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Kannada language)</span></dt><dd>Kannada is a Dravidian language spoken mainly in the Indian state of Karnataka, with more than 40 million speakers.</dd></div><div id="kapellmeister"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kapellmeister">Kapellmeister</a></dt><dd>Kapellmeister is a German title for the director of music at a princely court or church, originally the leader of a chapel's musicians.</dd></div><div id="kapudan-pasha"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kapudan-pasha">Kapudan Pasha</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Kapudan-ı Derya)</span></dt><dd>Kapudan Pasha was the title of the grand admiral of the Ottoman navy, from the 16th century one of the highest officials of the empire.</dd></div><div id="karmapa"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/karmapa">Karmapa</a></dt><dd>The Karmapa is the head of the Karma Kagyu school of Tibetan Buddhism and the oldest line of reincarnated lamas in Tibet, beginning with Düsum Khyenpa in the 12th century.</dd></div><div id="kebek-coin"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kebek-coin">Kebek (coin)</a> <span class="gl-aka">(kebeks)</span></dt><dd>The kebek was a standardised silver coin introduced by Kebek Khan of the Chagatai Khanate in the 1320s.</dd></div><div id="kenjutsu"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kenjutsu">Kenjutsu</a></dt><dd>Kenjutsu is the Japanese art of swordsmanship.</dd></div><div id="kente-cloth"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kente-cloth">Kente cloth</a> <span class="gl-aka">(kente)</span></dt><dd>Kente cloth is a brightly coloured woven textile of the Akan peoples of present-day Ghana, made from interwoven strips of cloth.</dd></div><div id="kerosene"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kerosene">Kerosene</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Kerosine)</span></dt><dd>Kerosene, also called paraffin, is a combustible hydrocarbon liquid distilled from petroleum and used as a fuel for lamps, stoves and jet engines; the distilling of crude oil into kerosene was first written about in the 9th century by the…</dd></div><div id="khalifa"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/khalifa">Khalifa</a></dt><dd>Khalifa, rendered in English as caliph, is the Arabic title for the successor to the Prophet Muhammad as leader of the Muslim community.</dd></div><div id="khedive-of-egypt"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/khedive-of-egypt">Khedive of Egypt</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Khedive)</span></dt><dd>Khedive was the title of the hereditary viceroys of Egypt under Ottoman suzerainty, granted by the sultan to Isma'il Pasha in 1867 and used until 1914.</dd></div><div id="khowar-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/khowar-language">Khowar language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Chitrali)</span></dt><dd>Khowar, also called Chitrali, is an Indo-Aryan language of the Dardic group spoken by several hundred thousand people in Chitral in Khyber Pakhtunkhwa, Pakistan, and in parts of Gilgit-Baltistan.</dd></div><div id="khwarazmshah"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmshah">Khwarazmshah</a></dt><dd>Khwarazmshah ('Shah of Khwarazm') was the title of the rulers of Khwarazm, the oasis region on the lower Amu Darya south of the Aral Sea.</dd></div><div id="kibushi"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kibushi">Kibushi</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Shibushi)</span></dt><dd>Kibushi is the Malagasy language spoken in Mayotte, closely related to the Sakalava dialect of north-western Madagascar.</dd></div><div id="kilo-class-submarine"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kilo-class-submarine">Kilo-class submarine</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Project 636)</span></dt><dd>The Kilo class is the NATO name for a type of diesel-electric attack submarine designed in the Soviet Union and built from the early 1980s, later in an improved version known as Project 636.</dd></div><div id="kinyarwanda"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kinyarwanda">Kinyarwanda</a></dt><dd>Kinyarwanda is the Bantu language of Rwanda, spoken by nearly the whole population and closely related to Kirundi of Burundi.</dd></div><div id="kipchak-languages"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kipchak-languages">Kipchak languages</a></dt><dd>The Kipchak languages are a branch of the Turkic languages spoken from eastern Europe to Central Asia, including Kazakh, Kyrgyz, Tatar, Bashkir, Karakalpak, Nogai and Crimean Tatar.</dd></div><div id="kiva"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kiva">Kiva</a></dt><dd>A kiva is a room used by the Pueblo peoples of the American South-West for rites and political meetings; among the Hopi and most other Pueblos it is a round, underground chamber.</dd></div><div id="kola-nut"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kola-nut">Kola nut</a> <span class="gl-aka">(kola nuts)</span></dt><dd>The kola nut is the caffeine-containing seed of the kola tree, native to the West African forest zone.</dd></div><div id="komtur"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/komtur">Komtur</a></dt><dd>Komtur, from Latin commendator, was the title of the commander of a commandery (Komturei) in the Teutonic Order and some other military orders.</dd></div><div id="kongo-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kongo-language">Kongo language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Kikongo)</span></dt><dd>Kongo, or Kikongo, is a Bantu language spoken by the Kongo people in western Democratic Republic of the Congo, northern Angola and the Republic of the Congo.</dd></div><div id="kopek"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kopek">Kopek</a></dt><dd>The kopek is a small unit of Russian currency, one hundredth of a rouble.</dd></div><div id="kopje"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kopje">Kopje</a></dt><dd>A kopje is a small, isolated hill, often a rocky granite outcrop, rising from the surrounding plain in southern Africa.</dd></div><div id="kora"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kora">Kora</a></dt><dd>The kora is a twenty-one-string harp-lute of the Mandinka and neighbouring peoples of West Africa, played above all by jali (griots), the hereditary musicians and keepers of oral history.</dd></div><div id="kshatriya"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a></dt><dd>Kshatriya is the second of the four varnas of the traditional Hindu social order, the class of rulers and warriors, ranked after the Brahmins.</dd></div><div id="kulinism"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kulinism">Kulinism</a></dt><dd>Kulinism was a system of ranking among Bengali Brahmins and Kayasthas that placed certain families, the Kulins, at the top of the social order.</dd></div><div id="kuraka"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kuraka">Kuraka</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Curaca)</span></dt><dd>A kuraka, or curaca, was an Andean aristocrat and magistrate who headed an ayllu or a federation of ayllus, an office often but not always inherited; under the Inca Empire his authority was granted by the Inca.</dd></div><div id="kurgan"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kurgan">Kurgan</a></dt><dd>A kurgan is a burial mound raised over a grave, a form of tomb common across the Eurasian steppe from prehistoric times.</dd></div><div id="kyabazinga"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/kyabazinga">Kyabazinga</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Kyabazinga of Busoga)</span></dt><dd>Kyabazinga is the title of the king of Busoga, in eastern Uganda.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-l"><h2>L</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="la-tene-culture"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture</a></dt><dd>The La Tène culture was the late Iron Age culture of Celtic Europe, from about 450 BCE until the Roman conquest.</dd></div><div id="lachit-divas"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/lachit-divas">Lachit Divas</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Lachit Diwas)</span></dt><dd>Lachit Divas is an annual day of commemoration held in Assam, India, on 24 November, the birthday of Lachit Borphukan, the Ahom general who defeated the Mughal army at the Battle of Saraighat in 1671.</dd></div><div id="lacquerware"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/lacquerware">Lacquerware</a></dt><dd>Lacquerware is objects coated with lacquer made from the sap of the lacquer tree, producing a durable, glossy finish.</dd></div><div id="lance"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/lance">Lance</a> <span class="gl-aka">(lancers)</span></dt><dd>A lance is a long spear designed for use by mounted soldiers, especially in the charge.</dd></div><div id="land-tenure"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/land-tenure">Land tenure</a> <span class="gl-aka">(land grant system)</span></dt><dd>Land tenure refers to the rules and arrangements by which land is held, used, granted and transferred.</dd></div><div id="landammann"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/landammann">Landammann</a></dt><dd>Landammann is the title of the chief magistrate in several Swiss cantons.</dd></div><div id="landammann-of-switzerland"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/landammann-of-switzerland">Landammann of Switzerland</a></dt><dd>The Landammann of Switzerland was the head of the Swiss Confederation under Napoleon's Act of Mediation from 1803 to 1813.</dd></div><div id="late-classic-period"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/late-classic-period">Late Classic period</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Late Classic Maya)</span></dt><dd>The Late Classic is the period of Maya history from about 600 to 900 CE, when lowland Maya civilisation reached its height.</dd></div><div id="lateen-sail"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/lateen-sail">Lateen sail</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Lateen, Latin rig)</span></dt><dd>A lateen is a triangular sail set on a long yard mounted at an angle on the mast and running fore and aft; it originated in the Mediterranean as early as the 2nd century CE, in Roman times.</dd></div><div id="latin"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a></dt><dd>Latin is the language of ancient Rome, which spread across the western Mediterranean with Roman rule and became the root of the Romance languages.</dd></div><div id="lavelua"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/lavelua">Lavelua</a></dt><dd>Lavelua is the title of the paramount king of Uvea, or Wallis Island, in the Pacific.</dd></div><div id="leather"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/leather">Leather</a> <span class="gl-aka">(leather goods)</span></dt><dd>Leather is animal hide tanned and prepared for making goods such as bags, sandals, saddles and book covers.</dd></div><div id="lepontic-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/lepontic-language">Lepontic language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Lepontic)</span></dt><dd>Lepontic is an ancient Celtic language known from about 140 inscriptions found around the Italian lakes and the southern Alps, dated from about the 6th to the 1st century BCE.</dd></div><div id="lex-rhodia"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/lex-rhodia">Lex Rhodia</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Rhodian Sea Law)</span></dt><dd>The Lex Rhodia was the maritime law of Rhodes on jettison: when cargo was thrown overboard to save a ship, the loss was shared among all whose property had been saved.</dd></div><div id="libyco-berber-script"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/libyco-berber-script">Libyco-Berber script</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Libyc script)</span></dt><dd>The Libyco-Berber script is an ancient alphabet of consonants used to write Berber languages across North Africa and the Canary Islands from at least the third century BCE.</dd></div><div id="linear-a"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a></dt><dd>Linear A is the undeciphered script used by the Minoans of Crete for administration and religious inscriptions from about 1800 to 1450 BCE.</dd></div><div id="linear-b"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a></dt><dd>Linear B is the syllabic script used by the Mycenaean palaces of Greece and Crete from about 1450 to 1200 BCE to record an early form of Greek.</dd></div><div id="linear-elamite"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/linear-elamite">Linear Elamite</a></dt><dd>Linear Elamite is an ancient script used in Elam in the late third millennium BCE.</dd></div><div id="lingua-franca"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">Lingua franca</a></dt><dd>A lingua franca is a language used for communication between speakers of different native languages.</dd></div><div id="litunga"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/litunga">Litunga</a></dt><dd>Litunga, meaning 'keeper of the earth', is the title of the king of the Lozi people of Barotseland in western Zambia.</dd></div><div id="llanito"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/llanito">Llanito</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Yanito)</span></dt><dd>Llanito is the vernacular speech of Gibraltar, an Andalusian Spanish base mixed heavily with British English through frequent code-switching.</dd></div><div id="lord-of-mann"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/lord-of-mann">Lord of Mann</a></dt><dd>Lord of Mann is the title of the ruler of the Isle of Man.</dd></div><div id="lord-of-the-isles"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/lord-of-the-isles">Lord of the Isles</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Dominus Insularum)</span></dt><dd>Lord of the Isles, in Latin Dominus Insularum, was the title of the Clan Donald rulers of the Hebrides and western Highlands, first used by John of Islay in 1336.</dd></div><div id="luganda"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/luganda">Luganda</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Ganda language)</span></dt><dd>Luganda is the Bantu language of the Baganda of central Uganda.</dd></div><div id="lukonkesh"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/lukonkesh">Lukonkesh</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Lukokesha)</span></dt><dd>Lukonkesh (Lukokesha) was the title of the queen mother of the Lunda Empire in central Africa, a female dignitary of high rank at the royal capital, the musumba.</dd></div><div id="luxembourgish"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/luxembourgish">Luxembourgish</a></dt><dd>Luxembourgish is a West Germanic language, derived from Moselle Franconian dialects, spoken as a mother tongue by most Luxembourgers.</dd></div><div id="lyciarch"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/lyciarch">Lyciarch</a></dt><dd>The Lyciarch was the president of the Lycian League, the federation of the cities of ancient Lycia in south-western Asia Minor.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-m"><h2>M</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="madhab"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/madhab">Madhab</a> <span class="gl-aka">(madhhab)</span></dt><dd>A madhab is a school of Islamic jurisprudence, a tradition of legal interpretation based on the Quran and Sunna.</dd></div><div id="magadhi-prakrit"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/magadhi-prakrit">Magadhi Prakrit</a></dt><dd>Magadhi Prakrit was the Middle Indo-Aryan vernacular of ancient Magadha in eastern India.</dd></div><div id="magahi"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/magahi">Magahi</a></dt><dd>Magahi is an Indo-Aryan language spoken by more than 12 million people, mainly in southern Bihar and neighbouring parts of Jharkhand in India.</dd></div><div id="magnetic-compass"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">Magnetic compass</a> <span class="gl-aka">(compass)</span></dt><dd>The magnetic compass is a navigational instrument that uses a magnetised needle to indicate direction.</dd></div><div id="magyarisation"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/magyarisation">Magyarisation</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Magyarization)</span></dt><dd>Magyarisation was the policy of spreading the Hungarian language and identity among the non-Hungarian peoples of the Kingdom of Hungary, pursued especially after the Compromise of 1867.</dd></div><div id="maharaja"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Maharajah)</span></dt><dd>Maharaja ('great king') is a Sanskrit royal title used across South Asia, originally for major sovereigns and later by many Hindu and Sikh rulers of princely states.</dd></div><div id="maharana"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/maharana">Maharana</a></dt><dd>Maharana, 'great rana', was the royal title of the rulers of Mewar, whose capital was Chittor and from 1559 Udaipur, and of a few other Rajput states such as Porbandar.</dd></div><div id="maize"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/maize">Maize</a></dt><dd>Maize is a cereal crop domesticated in Mesoamerica, the staple of the Aztec and other American civilisations.</dd></div><div id="malay-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/malay-language">Malay language</a></dt><dd>The Malay language is an Austronesian language spoken in the Malay Peninsula, Sumatra and much of maritime South-East Asia.</dd></div><div id="malayalam-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/malayalam-language">Malayalam language</a></dt><dd>The Malayalam language is a Dravidian language spoken mainly in Kerala and Lakshadweep in south-western India, by about 35 million people.</dd></div><div id="maloango"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/maloango">Maloango</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Ma Loango)</span></dt><dd>Maloango, or Ma Loango, was the title of the king of Loango on the Atlantic coast north of the Congo River.</dd></div><div id="maltese-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/maltese-language">Maltese language</a></dt><dd>Maltese is the national language of Malta and an official language of the European Union.</dd></div><div id="mandarin-chinese"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/mandarin-chinese">Mandarin Chinese</a></dt><dd>Mandarin Chinese is the largest group of Chinese dialects, spoken across northern and south-western China, and the basis of Standard Chinese, the official language of the People's Republic of China and Taiwan.</dd></div><div id="manilla"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/manilla">Manilla</a></dt><dd>A manilla was a horseshoe-shaped bracelet of copper or brass used as currency in West Africa.</dd></div><div id="manshur"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/manshur">Manshur</a></dt><dd>A manshur was a formal decree or diploma of investiture issued by a caliph.</dd></div><div id="manuscript"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscript</a></dt><dd>A manuscript is a handwritten book or document.</dd></div><div id="manuscript-illumination"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/manuscript-illumination">Manuscript illumination</a></dt><dd>Manuscript illumination is the decoration of handwritten books with painted images, ornament and gold.</dd></div><div id="manx-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/manx-language">Manx language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Manx Gaelic)</span></dt><dd>Manx is a Goidelic Celtic language of the Isle of Man, closely related to Irish and Scottish Gaelic.</dd></div><div id="marathi"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a></dt><dd>Marathi is an Indo-Aryan language spoken mainly in western India, in present-day Maharashtra.</dd></div><div id="marine-chronometer"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/marine-chronometer">Marine chronometer</a></dt><dd>A marine chronometer is a precision timepiece carried on a ship to find its longitude by comparing Greenwich Mean Time with local time, first developed in the 18th century: the Yorkshire carpenter John Harrison's H4 of 1761 solved the…</dd></div><div id="matchlock"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">Matchlock</a></dt><dd>The matchlock was an early firearm mechanism in which a slow-burning match was lowered onto the priming powder to fire the gun.</dd></div><div id="mawlid"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/mawlid">Mawlid</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Mawlid al-Nabi)</span></dt><dd>Mawlid is the celebration of the birthday of the Prophet Muhammad, held by many Muslims on 12 Rabi al-Awwal, the third month of the Islamic calendar.</dd></div><div id="maxim-gun"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/maxim-gun">Maxim gun</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Maxim guns)</span></dt><dd>The Maxim gun was the first fully automatic machine gun, invented by Hiram Maxim in the 1880s.</dd></div><div id="maya-blue"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/maya-blue">Maya blue</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Azul maya)</span></dt><dd>Maya blue is a turquoise or azure blue pigment made by cultures of pre-Columbian Mesoamerica, such as the Maya and Aztecs, from about 800 CE; it combines indigo dye from the leaves of the anil plant with palygorskite, a natural clay.</dd></div><div id="maya-script"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/maya-script">Maya script</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Maya glyphs, Maya hieroglyphs)</span></dt><dd>Maya script, or Maya glyphs, is the writing system of the Maya civilisation of Mesoamerica, known from inscriptions of the 3rd century BCE at late Preclassic sites such as San Bartolo in Guatemala and Chakjobon in Mexico.</dd></div><div id="measles"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/measles">Measles</a></dt><dd>Measles is a highly contagious viral disease.</dd></div><div id="mechanical-clock"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/mechanical-clock">Mechanical clock</a></dt><dd>A mechanical clock is a timekeeping device driven by a mechanism such as falling weights or water and an escapement.</dd></div><div id="medism"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/medism">Medism</a></dt><dd>Medism was the Greek term for collaborating with or siding with the Persian Empire, whose people the Greeks often called Medes.</dd></div><div id="mefo-bills"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/mefo-bills">Mefo bills</a></dt><dd>Mefo bills were promissory notes devised by Hjalmar Schacht to finance German rearmament in secret from 1934.</dd></div><div id="mehri-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/mehri-language">Mehri language</a></dt><dd>Mehri is a Modern South Arabian language spoken by the Mahra people of eastern Yemen and western Oman, and by migrants in the Gulf states.</dd></div><div id="mercenary"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">Mercenary</a></dt><dd>A mercenary is a soldier who fights for pay in the service of a foreign power.</dd></div><div id="meroitic-script"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/meroitic-script">Meroitic script</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Meroitic)</span></dt><dd>The Meroitic script was the writing system of the Kingdom of Kush in its Meroitic period, used from about the second century BCE.</dd></div><div id="mesoamerican-long-count-calendar"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerican-long-count-calendar">Mesoamerican Long Count calendar</a></dt><dd>The Mesoamerican Long Count calendar counts the days elapsed since a fixed starting date in 3114 BCE, using units based on twenty.</dd></div><div id="messapic-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/messapic-language">Messapic language</a></dt><dd>Messapic was an extinct Indo-European language of the Messapians of south-eastern Italy, in the Salento peninsula of Apulia.</dd></div><div id="microscope"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/microscope">Microscope</a></dt><dd>A microscope is an instrument for examining objects too small to be seen by the naked eye; the earliest known compound microscopes, which combine an objective lens near the specimen with an eyepiece, appeared in Europe around 1620.</dd></div><div id="middle-persian"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Pahlavi)</span></dt><dd>Middle Persian, also called Pahlavi, was the Iranian language of the Sasanian Empire, descended from Old Persian and ancestral to modern Persian.</dd></div><div id="military-industrial-complex"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/military-industrial-complex">Military-industrial complex</a></dt><dd>The military-industrial complex is the network of armed forces, arms manufacturers and political interests that grows up around a permanent defence establishment.</dd></div><div id="millet"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/millet">Millet</a></dt><dd>Millet is a group of hardy, drought-tolerant cereal grains grown widely in Africa and Asia.</dd></div><div id="minaic-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/minaic-language">Minaic language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Minaean language)</span></dt><dd>Minaic was the Ancient South Arabian language of the Kingdom of Ma'in in the al-Jawf region of Yemen, written in the South Arabian script.</dd></div><div id="minie-rifle"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/minie-rifle">Minié rifle</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Minié rifles)</span></dt><dd>The Minie rifle was a muzzle-loading rifled musket firing a conical bullet that expanded into the barrel's rifling, allowing accurate long-range fire.</dd></div><div id="minnesang"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/minnesang">Minnesang</a></dt><dd>Minnesang was the tradition of courtly love poetry and song in medieval Germany, flourishing from the twelfth to the fourteenth century.</dd></div><div id="mixed-member-proportional-representation"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/mixed-member-proportional-representation">Mixed-member proportional representation</a> <span class="gl-aka">(MMP)</span></dt><dd>Mixed-member proportional representation, or MMP, is an electoral system in which voters elect constituency members and also vote for parties, with list seats allocated so that each party's share of parliament matches its share of the…</dd></div><div id="mobile-phone"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/mobile-phone">Mobile phone</a> <span class="gl-aka">(cell phone, mobile telephone)</span></dt><dd>A mobile phone is a portable telephone that makes and receives calls over a radio link while its user moves about; the first handheld mobile phone was demonstrated by Martin Cooper of Motorola in New York City on 3 April 1973, with a…</dd></div><div id="mogho-naba"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/mogho-naba">Mogho Naba</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Moro Naba, Mogho Naaba)</span></dt><dd>The Mogho Naba ('ruler of the world') is the title of the king of the Mossi kingdom of Ouagadougou in present-day Burkina Faso, the senior of the Mossi states.</dd></div><div id="monsoon"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">Monsoon</a></dt><dd>The monsoon is a seasonal reversal of winds that brings heavy summer rains to South and South-East Asia.</dd></div><div id="mounted-archer"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">Mounted archer</a> <span class="gl-aka">(mounted archers)</span></dt><dd>A mounted archer is a cavalry soldier who shoots arrows from horseback.</dd></div><div id="movable-type"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/movable-type">Movable type</a></dt><dd>Movable type is a printing system using individual, reusable characters that can be arranged to form texts.</dd></div><div id="mudbrick"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">Mudbrick</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Mud-brick, Adobe)</span></dt><dd>A mudbrick is an air-dried, unfired brick of mud mixed with a binder such as straw or rice husks, first known from the dwellings of Jericho, dated to 9000 BCE.</dd></div><div id="muharram"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/muharram">Muharram</a></dt><dd>Muharram is the first month of the Islamic lunar calendar and one of its four sacred months.</dd></div><div id="mulena-mukwae"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/mulena-mukwae">Mulena Mukwae</a></dt><dd>The Mulena Mukwae is the title of the royal princess who rules the southern part of Barotseland from Nalolo, under the Litunga, the Lozi king, who rules from the north.</dd></div><div id="muqarnas"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/muqarnas">Muqarnas</a></dt><dd>Muqarnas is a form of architectural ornament in Islamic architecture consisting of tiers of small niches, creating a honeycomb or stalactite effect in vaults and domes.</dd></div><div id="musket"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/musket">Musket</a> <span class="gl-aka">(musketeer)</span></dt><dd>The musket is a muzzle-loading, smoothbore firearm that became the standard infantry weapon in many armies from the sixteenth to the nineteenth century.</dd></div><div id="mutasa"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/mutasa">Mutasa</a></dt><dd>Mutasa is the hereditary title of the rulers of Manyika, a Shona kingdom in the highlands on the border of present-day Zimbabwe and Mozambique.</dd></div><div id="mwamba-bemba-title"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/mwamba-bemba-title">Mwamba (Bemba title)</a></dt><dd>Mwamba is the title of one of the senior chiefs of the Bemba of north-eastern Zambia, who rules a large territory next to that of the Chitimukulu, the paramount.</dd></div><div id="mwata-yamvo"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/mwata-yamvo">Mwata Yamvo</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Mwaant Yav)</span></dt><dd>Mwata Yamvo is the title of the paramount ruler of the Lunda Empire, centred in the south of the present Democratic Republic of the Congo.</dd></div><div id="myrrh"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/myrrh">Myrrh</a></dt><dd>Myrrh is an aromatic resin obtained from trees native to north-east Africa and southern Arabia, prized in antiquity for incense, perfume and embalming.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-n"><h2>N</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="nahuatl"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a></dt><dd>Nahuatl is a Uto-Aztecan language spoken by the Mexica and other peoples of central Mexico.</dd></div><div id="nasi-title"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/nasi-title">Nasi (title)</a></dt><dd>Nasi is a Hebrew title meaning 'prince' or 'leader', used in the Hebrew Bible for tribal chiefs.</dd></div><div id="national-debt"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/national-debt">National debt</a> <span class="gl-aka">(funded national debt)</span></dt><dd>A national debt is the total borrowing of a state, typically funded through government bonds.</dd></div><div id="national-hero-of-indonesia"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/national-hero-of-indonesia">National Hero of Indonesia</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Pahlawan Nasional)</span></dt><dd>National Hero of Indonesia (Pahlawan Nasional) is the highest honorary title awarded by the Indonesian government, given posthumously since 1959 to people who fought colonial rule or served the nation.</dd></div><div id="national-redoubt-switzerland"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/national-redoubt-switzerland">National Redoubt (Switzerland)</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Réduit national)</span></dt><dd>The National Redoubt was the Swiss defence plan announced by General Henri Guisan in July 1940, after the fall of France left Switzerland almost surrounded by the Axis.</dd></div><div id="natron"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/natron">Natron</a></dt><dd>Natron is a naturally occurring mixture of sodium salts, used historically in cleaning, preservation, glassmaking and medicine.</dd></div><div id="naval-blockade"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">Naval blockade</a> <span class="gl-aka">(blockade)</span></dt><dd>A naval blockade is the use of warships to stop shipping from reaching or leaving an enemy's ports.</dd></div><div id="nayaka"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Nayak)</span></dt><dd>Nayaka, meaning leader or chief, was a title for military commanders in medieval South India.</dd></div><div id="ndlovukati"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ndlovukati">Ndlovukati</a></dt><dd>Ndlovukati, meaning she-elephant, is the title of the queen mother of the Swazi Kingdom (Eswatini).</dd></div><div id="ndonga-dialect"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ndonga-dialect">Ndonga dialect</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Oshindonga)</span></dt><dd>Oshindonga is a dialect of the Ovambo language spoken by the Ndonga people of northern Namibia.</dd></div><div id="neopalatial-period"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/neopalatial-period">Neopalatial period</a> <span class="gl-aka">(New Palace period)</span></dt><dd>The Neopalatial or New Palace period is the high point of Minoan civilisation on Crete, from about 1700 to 1450 BCE, when the palaces of Knossos, Phaistos, Malia and Zakros were rebuilt on a grander scale and Minoan art, trade and…</dd></div><div id="nepal-sambat"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/nepal-sambat">Nepal Sambat</a></dt><dd>Nepal Sambat is a lunar calendar era of Nepal that begins in 879 CE.</dd></div><div id="new-economic-mechanism"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/new-economic-mechanism">New Economic Mechanism</a></dt><dd>New Economic Mechanism is the name of market-oriented reforms in two communist states.</dd></div><div id="newspaper"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/newspaper">Newspaper</a></dt><dd>A newspaper is a periodical publication of written news about current events; the German-language Relation, printed from 1605 by Johann Carolus in Strasbourg, in the Holy Roman Empire, is often recognised as the first.</dd></div><div id="ngambela"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ngambela">Ngambela</a></dt><dd>The Ngambela is the chief councillor, or prime minister, of the Lozi kingdom of Barotseland.</dd></div><div id="ngwenyama"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ngwenyama">Ngwenyama</a></dt><dd>Ngwenyama, meaning lion, is the title of the king of the Swazi Kingdom (Eswatini).</dd></div><div id="nicard-dialect"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/nicard-dialect">Niçard dialect</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Nissart, Niçard Occitan)</span></dt><dd>Niçard, or Nissart, is the variety of Occitan traditionally spoken in Nice and its former county, usually classed as a sub-dialect of Provençal.</dd></div><div id="ninja"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ninja">Ninja</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Shinobi)</span></dt><dd>Ninja, or shinobi, were warriors of pre-modern Japan who specialised in espionage, scouting, infiltration and surprise attacks, especially in the Sengoku period of the 15th and 16th centuries.</dd></div><div id="nobiin"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/nobiin">Nobiin</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Nobiin language, Mahas language)</span></dt><dd>Nobiin is a Nubian language spoken along the Nile in southern Egypt and northern Sudan, the language of the Fadicca and Mahas Nubians.</dd></div><div id="norman-architecture"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/norman-architecture">Norman architecture</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Norman Romanesque)</span></dt><dd>Norman architecture is the Romanesque style of building developed in Normandy in the eleventh century and carried to England after 1066 and to southern Italy and Sicily.</dd></div><div id="norn-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/norn-language">Norn language</a></dt><dd>Norn was the North Germanic language spoken in Orkney, Shetland and Caithness, descended from the Old Norse of the Viking settlers.</dd></div><div id="north-african-elephant"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/north-african-elephant">North African elephant</a> <span class="gl-aka">(North African forest elephant)</span></dt><dd>The North African elephant was a population of elephants, now extinct, that lived in north-western Africa in antiquity.</dd></div><div id="northern-black-polished-ware"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/northern-black-polished-ware">Northern Black Polished Ware</a></dt><dd>Northern Black Polished Ware is a fine, glossy black or grey pottery of the Iron Age in northern India, made from about 700 to 200 BCE.</dd></div><div id="northern-thai-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/northern-thai-language">Northern Thai language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Kham Mueang, Lanna language)</span></dt><dd>Northern Thai, known to its speakers as Kham Mueang, is the Tai language of the former kingdom of Lan Na, spoken by several million people in northern Thailand.</dd></div><div id="nuclear-fission"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/nuclear-fission">Nuclear fission</a></dt><dd>Nuclear fission is a reaction in which the nucleus of an atom splits into two or more smaller nuclei, releasing a very large amount of energy; it was discovered in Berlin in December 1938, when the chemists Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann…</dd></div><div id="nutmeg"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">Nutmeg</a></dt><dd>Nutmeg is a spice obtained from the seed of a tree native to the Banda Islands in the Moluccas of present-day Indonesia.</dd></div><div id="nylon"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/nylon">Nylon</a></dt><dd>Nylon is a family of synthetic polymers joined by amide links, first made as nylon 66 by Wallace Hume Carothers at the DuPont Experimental Station on 28 February 1935.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-o"><h2>O</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="oba-of-lagos"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/oba-of-lagos">Oba of Lagos</a></dt><dd>The Oba of Lagos is the traditional ruler of Lagos, Nigeria.</dd></div><div id="obelisk"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">Obelisk</a></dt><dd>An obelisk is a tall, narrow, tapering stone monument, often carved from a single block.</dd></div><div id="obong-of-calabar"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/obong-of-calabar">Obong of Calabar</a></dt><dd>The Obong of Calabar is the title of the paramount traditional ruler of the Efik people of Calabar in south-eastern Nigeria.</dd></div><div id="odia-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/odia-language">Odia language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Oriya)</span></dt><dd>Odia, formerly spelled Oriya, is an Indo-Aryan language spoken mainly in Odisha, India, written in its own rounded script.</dd></div><div id="oil-palm"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/oil-palm">Oil palm</a></dt><dd>The oil palm is a tree native to West and Central Africa whose fruit yields palm oil, an important food and trade product.</dd></div><div id="oil-well"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/oil-well">Oil well</a></dt><dd>An oil well is a hole bored into the Earth to bring petroleum to the surface; the earliest known were drilled in China in 347 CE.</dd></div><div id="old-french"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a></dt><dd>Old French is the form of the French language spoken from around the ninth to the fourteenth century.</dd></div><div id="old-high-german"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/old-high-german">Old High German</a></dt><dd>Old High German is the earliest recorded stage of the German language, used from about the eighth to the eleventh century.</dd></div><div id="old-javanese"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Kawi)</span></dt><dd>Old Javanese, or Kawi, is the literary and administrative language of Java recorded from the late 8th century, heavily influenced by Sanskrit.</dd></div><div id="old-kannada"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/old-kannada">Old Kannada</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Halegannada)</span></dt><dd>Old Kannada, or Halegannada, is the form of the Kannada language used in inscriptions and literature of the later first and early second millennium CE, from the Rashtrakuta period to the Hoysalas.</dd></div><div id="old-khmer"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/old-khmer">Old Khmer</a></dt><dd>Old Khmer is the earliest recorded form of the Khmer language, used in inscriptions in Cambodia from the seventh century.</dd></div><div id="old-malay"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/old-malay">Old Malay</a></dt><dd>Old Malay is the earliest attested form of the Malay language, known from inscriptions in Sumatra and nearby regions.</dd></div><div id="old-mon"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/old-mon">Old Mon</a></dt><dd>Old Mon is the earliest recorded form of the Mon language, an Austroasiatic language of mainland Southeast Asia.</dd></div><div id="old-nubian"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/old-nubian">Old Nubian</a></dt><dd>Old Nubian is the medieval written form of the Nubian language, used in the Christian kingdoms of the middle Nile from about the 8th to the 15th century.</dd></div><div id="old-persian"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a></dt><dd>Old Persian is the ancient Iranian language of the Achaemenid kings, written in a cuneiform script.</dd></div><div id="old-turkic"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/old-turkic">Old Turkic</a></dt><dd>Old Turkic is the earliest recorded Turkic language, written in a runic script from the 8th century.</dd></div><div id="old-uyghur-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/old-uyghur-language">Old Uyghur language</a></dt><dd>Old Uyghur was the Turkic language of the Uyghur Kingdom of Qocho and the Gansu Uyghurs from the 9th to the 14th centuries.</dd></div><div id="olive-oil"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">Olive oil</a></dt><dd>Olive oil is the oil pressed from olives, a staple of Mediterranean diet, lighting and trade since antiquity.</dd></div><div id="olowo"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/olowo">Olowo</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Olowo of Owo)</span></dt><dd>The Olowo is the title of the king of Owo, a Yoruba kingdom in Ondo State, Nigeria.</dd></div><div id="olu-of-warri"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/olu-of-warri">Olu of Warri</a></dt><dd>The Olu of Warri is the title of the king of the Itsekiri people of the western Niger Delta in Nigeria.</dd></div><div id="dong-son-culture"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/dong-son-culture">Đông Sơn culture</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Dong Son culture)</span></dt><dd>The Đông Sơn culture was a Bronze Age culture of the Red River and Ma River valleys of northern Vietnam, named after a village in Thanh Hóa province.</dd></div><div id="onojie"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/onojie">Onojie</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Enijie)</span></dt><dd>Onojie, plural Enijie, is the title of the hereditary traditional rulers of the Esan chiefdoms in Edo State, Nigeria.</dd></div><div id="optical-telegraph"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/optical-telegraph">Optical telegraph</a> <span class="gl-aka">(semaphore telegraph)</span></dt><dd>An optical telegraph is a line of stations, typically towers, that pass text along by visual signals; credit for the first successful one goes to the French engineer Claude Chappe and his brothers in 1792, and Chappe's word télégraphe…</dd></div><div id="oracle-bone-script"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/oracle-bone-script">Oracle bone script</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Jiaguwen)</span></dt><dd>The oracle bone script is the oldest attested form of written Chinese, carved mostly on ox shoulder bones and turtle plastrons for the Late Shang royal family from the 13th century BCE; inscriptions of King Wu Ding's reign are radiocarbon…</dd></div><div id="oral-rehydration-therapy"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/oral-rehydration-therapy">Oral rehydration therapy</a></dt><dd>Oral rehydration therapy treats dehydration, especially from diarrhoea, with water containing modest amounts of sugar and salts; the earliest known use was reported by Hemendra Nath Chatterjee in The Lancet in 1953, for patients with mild…</dd></div><div id="oscan-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/oscan-language">Oscan language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Oscan)</span></dt><dd>Oscan was an Italic language spoken in southern Italy by the Samnites, Campanians, Lucanians and related peoples in the second half of the first millennium BCE.</dd></div><div id="osco-umbrian-languages"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/osco-umbrian-languages">Osco-Umbrian languages</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Sabellic languages)</span></dt><dd>The Osco-Umbrian languages, also called Sabellic, are a group of extinct Italic languages once spoken across central and southern Italy.</dd></div><div id="osmanya"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/osmanya">Osmanya</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Osmanya script)</span></dt><dd>Osmanya is an alphabet for the Somali language created around 1920 by Osman Yusuf Kenadid, son of the sultan of Hobyo.</dd></div><div id="ostracism"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ostracism">Ostracism</a></dt><dd>Ostracism was an Athenian procedure by which the Assembly could exile a leading citizen for ten years without loss of property, by writing his name on potsherds, ostraka.</dd></div><div id="ostrich-feather"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ostrich-feather">Ostrich feather</a> <span class="gl-aka">(ostrich feathers)</span></dt><dd>Ostrich feathers were a luxury trade good valued for decoration and fashion, carried north from the Sahel and Sahara across trans-Saharan routes.</dd></div><div id="oto-manguean-languages"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/oto-manguean-languages">Oto-Manguean languages</a></dt><dd>The Oto-Manguean languages are a large family of Indigenous languages of Mexico and Central America, including Zapotec, Mixtec, Otomi, Mazahua and Chinantec.</dd></div><div id="outrigger-canoe"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/outrigger-canoe">Outrigger canoe</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Outrigger boat)</span></dt><dd>An outrigger canoe is a boat steadied by one or more side floats, or outriggers, fastened to its hull; such boats were developed by the Austronesian-speaking peoples of the islands of Southeast Asia, and they enabled the Austronesian…</dd></div><div id="overgrazing"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/overgrazing">Overgrazing</a></dt><dd>Overgrazing occurs when livestock graze pasture more heavily than it can recover, degrading the land.</dd></div><div id="owa-obokun"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/owa-obokun">Owa Obokun</a></dt><dd>Owa Obokun is the title of the king of Ijesha, a Yoruba kingdom in south-western Nigeria whose capital is Ilesa.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-p"><h2>P</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="painted-grey-ware-culture"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/painted-grey-ware-culture">Painted Grey Ware culture</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Painted Grey Ware)</span></dt><dd>The Painted Grey Ware culture is an Iron Age archaeological culture of the western Ganges plain and the Ghaggar-Hakra valley in northern India, dated to roughly 1200–600 BCE.</dd></div><div id="palayakkarar"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/palayakkarar">Palayakkarar</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Poligars, Poligar)</span></dt><dd>The Palayakkarar, known in English as Poligars and in Kannada as Palegars, were military chiefs of south India who held territories called palayams under the Vijayanagara Empire and its successor Nayaka states in return for military…</dd></div><div id="pallava-script"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pallava-script">Pallava script</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Pallava Grantha)</span></dt><dd>The Pallava script is a Brahmic script that developed in southern India under the Pallava dynasty from around the 4th century CE.</dd></div><div id="palm-oil"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">Palm oil</a></dt><dd>Palm oil is an edible oil made from the fruit of the oil palm, a tree native to West and Central Africa.</dd></div><div id="pant-pratinidhi"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pant-pratinidhi">Pant Pratinidhi</a></dt><dd>Pant Pratinidhi, or Pratinidhi, was a high office of the Maratha state, created by Chhatrapati Rajaram in the 1690s for a viceregent who could act in the king's name during the wars with the Mughals.</dd></div><div id="pant-sachiv"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pant-sachiv">Pant Sachiv</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Sachiv)</span></dt><dd>The Sachiv, or Pant Sachiv, was one of the eight ministers of the Ashta Pradhan council created by Shivaji, responsible for royal correspondence and records.</dd></div><div id="paper-money"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">Paper money</a></dt><dd>Paper money is currency printed on paper rather than minted in metal.</dd></div><div id="papermaking"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/papermaking">Papermaking</a> <span class="gl-aka">(paper-making)</span></dt><dd>Papermaking is the craft of producing paper from plant fibres, invented in China.</dd></div><div id="papiamento"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/papiamento">Papiamento</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Papiamentu)</span></dt><dd>Papiamento is a creole language spoken on Aruba, Bonaire and Curaçao in the southern Caribbean.</dd></div><div id="papyrus"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">Papyrus</a></dt><dd>Papyrus is a writing material made from the pith of the papyrus plant, which grew abundantly in the Nile valley and delta.</dd></div><div id="parthian-shot"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/parthian-shot">Parthian shot</a></dt><dd>The Parthian shot was a mounted archery tactic in which a horse archer, real or feigning retreat, turned in the saddle to shoot at pursuing enemies.</dd></div><div id="partible-inheritance"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/partible-inheritance">Partible inheritance</a></dt><dd>Partible inheritance is a custom by which a father's property is divided among all his sons rather than passing to a single heir.</dd></div><div id="pashto"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pashto">Pashto</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Pashto language)</span></dt><dd>Pashto is an Eastern Iranian language spoken by the Pashtuns of Afghanistan and north-western Pakistan, written in a modified Arabic script.</dd></div><div id="passover"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Pesach)</span></dt><dd>Passover is a major Jewish festival commemorating the Exodus of the Israelites from Egypt, celebrated in spring from the 15th day of the month of Nisan.</dd></div><div id="pasteurisation"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pasteurisation">Pasteurisation</a> <span class="gl-aka">(pasteurization)</span></dt><dd>Pasteurisation is the preservation of foods such as milk and fruit juice by mild heat, usually below 100 °C, to kill pathogens and extend shelf life; it is named after the French chemist Louis Pasteur, who found in 1864, at Arbois, that…</dd></div><div id="pastoralism"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pastoralism">Pastoralism</a> <span class="gl-aka">(pastoral)</span></dt><dd>Pastoralism is a way of life based on herding livestock such as cattle, sheep, goats, horses and camels, often involving seasonal movement between pastures.</dd></div><div id="patroon"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/patroon">Patroon</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Patroonship)</span></dt><dd>A patroon was a landholder in New Netherland granted a large estate, a patroonship, by the Dutch West India Company under its Charter of Freedoms and Exemptions of 1629, in return for settling fifty adults there within four years.</dd></div><div id="peanut"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/peanut">Peanut</a> <span class="gl-aka">(peanuts)</span></dt><dd>The peanut is a legume native to South America that spread worldwide after the Columbian exchange.</dd></div><div id="penal-transportation"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/penal-transportation">Penal transportation</a></dt><dd>Penal transportation was the punishment of sending convicted criminals to a distant colony.</dd></div><div id="pendulum-clock"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pendulum-clock">Pendulum clock</a></dt><dd>A pendulum clock is a clock that uses a swinging weight, the pendulum, as its timekeeping element; it was invented on 25 December 1656 by the Dutch scientist Christiaan Huygens, inspired by Galileo Galilei's investigations of pendulums…</dd></div><div id="penicillin"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/penicillin">Penicillin</a></dt><dd>Penicillin is a β-lactam antibiotic first obtained from Penicillium moulds; it was discovered in 1928 by the Scottish physician Alexander Fleming at St Mary's Hospital in London, who saw that mould contaminating a bacterial culture…</dd></div><div id="peoples-commissar"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/peoples-commissar">People's commissar</a></dt><dd>A people's commissar was a minister of the Soviet government between 1917 and 1946, a member of the Council of People's Commissars in charge of a branch of administration such as war, foreign affairs or nationalities.</dd></div><div id="pepper"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pepper">Pepper</a></dt><dd>Pepper is a spice made from the dried berries of a vine native to southern India, long one of the most valuable goods of Indian Ocean trade.</dd></div><div id="percheron"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/percheron">Percheron</a></dt><dd>The Percheron is a breed of heavy draught horse from the Perche region of north-western France, usually grey or black.</dd></div><div id="persian-carpet"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/persian-carpet">Persian carpet</a> <span class="gl-aka">(carpet-weaving)</span></dt><dd>The Persian carpet is a hand-knotted rug made in Iran, part of a weaving tradition stretching back many centuries.</dd></div><div id="persian-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/persian-language">Persian language</a></dt><dd>Persian is an Iranian language spoken mainly in Iran, Afghanistan and Tajikistan, whose literary form developed after the Islamic conquest from earlier Middle Persian.</dd></div><div id="persian-miniature"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">Persian miniature</a> <span class="gl-aka">(miniature painting)</span></dt><dd>Persian miniature is a tradition of small, detailed painting, usually used to illustrate manuscripts of poetry and history.</dd></div><div id="phags-pa-script"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/phags-pa-script">ʼPhags-pa script</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Phagspa script)</span></dt><dd>The ʼPhags-pa script is an alphabet created by the Tibetan lama Drogön Chögyal Phagpa at the request of Kublai Khan.</dd></div><div id="phalanx"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">Phalanx</a></dt><dd>A phalanx is a dense formation of heavily armed infantry fighting in close ranks.</dd></div><div id="phoenician-alphabet"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet">Phoenician alphabet</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Phoenician script)</span></dt><dd>The Phoenician alphabet is a script of 22 consonant letters, written from right to left, used in Phoenicia and across the Mediterranean for most of the 1st millennium BCE.</dd></div><div id="phosphate"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/phosphate">Phosphate</a> <span class="gl-aka">(phosphates)</span></dt><dd>Phosphate is a mineral resource used chiefly to make agricultural fertilisers.</dd></div><div id="photography"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/photography">Photography</a></dt><dd>Photography, whose name means 'drawing of light', is the capture of camera images on light-sensitive materials; the first such image to be fixed was made by the French scientist Nicéphore Niépce in 1826 or 1827, after an exposure of hours…</dd></div><div id="phra-khlang"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/phra-khlang">Phra Khlang</a></dt><dd>The Phra Khlang was the Siamese minister of the treasury, whose department under the Ayutthaya and early Bangkok kings also managed royal trade, the ports and relations with foreign merchants and states.</dd></div><div id="pike"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pike">Pike</a> <span class="gl-aka">(pikemen)</span></dt><dd>The pike is a long thrusting spear used by infantry in close formations, especially to resist cavalry.</dd></div><div id="pinot-noir"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pinot-noir">Pinot noir</a></dt><dd>Pinot noir is a red wine grape associated above all with Burgundy, where it has been grown since at least the fourteenth century.</dd></div><div id="piracy"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/piracy">Piracy</a> <span class="gl-aka">(pirates)</span></dt><dd>Piracy is robbery and violence committed at sea.</dd></div><div id="pitkern"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pitkern">Pitkern</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Pitcairnese)</span></dt><dd>Pitkern is a creole language based on 18th-century English and Tahitian, spoken by the inhabitants of Pitcairn Island.</dd></div><div id="plough"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/plough">Plough</a></dt><dd>A plough is a farm tool for loosening or turning soil before sowing; ploughs were at first powered by people, and the larger ox-drawn ard, or scratch plough, followed the domestication of oxen in Mesopotamia and the Indus Valley, perhaps…</dd></div><div id="poison-gas"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/poison-gas">Poison gas</a></dt><dd>Poison gas is a chemical weapon that injures or kills by inhalation or contact.</dd></div><div id="polio-vaccine"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/polio-vaccine">Polio vaccine</a></dt><dd>Polio vaccines prevent poliomyelitis and come in two types, an inactivated virus given by injection and a weakened virus given by mouth; Hilary Koprowski first tested a live, attenuated oral vaccine successfully in New York in 1950.</dd></div><div id="polonisation"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/polonisation">Polonisation</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Polonization)</span></dt><dd>Polonisation is the adoption of the Polish language, culture and often Catholic religion by non-Polish groups.</dd></div><div id="porcelain"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">Porcelain</a></dt><dd>Porcelain is a fine, hard, translucent ceramic first developed in China.</dd></div><div id="porfiriato"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/porfiriato">Porfiriato</a></dt><dd>The Porfiriato was the period of Porfirio Díaz's rule in Mexico, from 1876 to 1911, with an interval from 1880 to 1884.</dd></div><div id="portuguese-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-language">Portuguese language</a></dt><dd>Portuguese is a Romance language that originated in the western Iberian Peninsula.</dd></div><div id="potato"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/potato">Potato</a></dt><dd>The potato is a starchy tuber native to the Andes of South America, spread worldwide after the Spanish conquest.</dd></div><div id="potters-wheel"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/potters-wheel">Potter's wheel</a> <span class="gl-aka">(pottery wheel)</span></dt><dd>The potter's wheel is a machine for shaping, or throwing, clay into round ceramic ware; the oldest found, from the Cucuteni–Trypillia culture in western Ukraine, dates to the middle of the 5th millennium BCE.</dd></div><div id="pottery"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pottery">Pottery</a></dt><dd>Pottery is the craft and products of forming vessels and other objects from clay fired at high temperatures; the earliest known pottery vessels, from Xianren Cave in Jiangxi, China, date to 20,000–19,000 years ago.</dd></div><div id="pound-lock"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pound-lock">Pound lock</a></dt><dd>A pound lock is a chamber with gates at both ends in which the water level is raised or lowered to move boats between levels of a waterway, first built in Song dynasty China by Qiao Weiyue in 984.</dd></div><div id="pound-sterling"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pound-sterling">Pound sterling</a> <span class="gl-aka">(sterling)</span></dt><dd>The pound sterling is the currency of the United Kingdom.</dd></div><div id="prakrit"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Prakrits)</span></dt><dd>Prakrit is the name for the vernacular Middle Indo-Aryan languages of ancient India, related to Sanskrit but simpler in sound and grammar.</dd></div><div id="prefect-of-egypt"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/prefect-of-egypt">Prefect of Egypt</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Praefectus Aegypti)</span></dt><dd>The prefect of Egypt (praefectus Aegypti) was the governor of the Roman province of Egypt from its annexation in 30 BCE.</dd></div><div id="primogeniture"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">Primogeniture</a></dt><dd>Primogeniture is the rule of inheritance by which the eldest son succeeds to the whole of a title or estate.</dd></div><div id="prince-of-transylvania"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-transylvania">Prince of Transylvania</a></dt><dd>Prince of Transylvania was the title of the rulers of the Principality of Transylvania, first taken by John Sigismund Zápolya when he gave up the Hungarian royal title at the Treaty of Speyer in 1570.</dd></div><div id="prince-of-wales"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a></dt><dd>Prince of Wales was the title claimed by native Welsh rulers of Gwynedd as overlords of Wales.</dd></div><div id="printing-press"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">Printing press</a> <span class="gl-aka">(printing)</span></dt><dd>Printing is the mechanical reproduction of text and images.</dd></div><div id="pronoia"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pronoia">Pronoia</a> <span class="gl-aka">(pronoiai)</span></dt><dd>Pronoia, Greek for &quot;care&quot; or &quot;forethought&quot;, was a grant of state revenue in the late Byzantine Empire, from the 11th century to the 15th.</dd></div><div id="protective-tariff"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/protective-tariff">Protective tariff</a> <span class="gl-aka">(protective tariffs)</span></dt><dd>A protective tariff is a tax on imported goods designed to shield domestic producers from foreign competition.</dd></div><div id="proto-sinaitic-script"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/proto-sinaitic-script">Proto-Sinaitic script</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Early Alphabetic)</span></dt><dd>The Proto-Sinaitic script is the earliest known trace of alphabetic writing, found in about 30–40 inscriptions at Serabit el-Khadim in Sinai and in two at Wadi el-Hol in Middle Egypt dated to between 1900 and 1800 BCE.</dd></div><div id="protogeometric-style"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/protogeometric-style">Protogeometric style</a></dt><dd>The Protogeometric style is the style of Greek pottery of about 1050–900 BCE, after the collapse of Mycenaean civilisation, decorated with concentric circles and semicircles drawn with compasses and multiple brushes.</dd></div><div id="protopalatial-period"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/protopalatial-period">Protopalatial period</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Old Palace period)</span></dt><dd>The Protopalatial or Old Palace period is the phase of Minoan Crete, roughly 1900 to 1700 BCE, in which the first palaces were built at Knossos, Phaistos and Malia.</dd></div><div id="psychological-warfare"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/psychological-warfare">Psychological warfare</a></dt><dd>Psychological warfare is the use of fear, deception and propaganda to weaken an enemy's will to resist.</dd></div><div id="pulaar"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pulaar">Pulaar</a></dt><dd>Pulaar is the variety of the Fula language spoken in the Senegal River valley, the old region of Futa Toro, and in Senegal, Mauritania, the Gambia and Guinea-Bissau.</dd></div><div id="pulses"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pulses">Pulses</a></dt><dd>Pulses are the edible seeds of leguminous plants, such as lentils, peas, chickpeas and beans.</dd></div><div id="pyidawtha"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pyidawtha">Pyidawtha</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Pyidawtha Plan)</span></dt><dd>Pyidawtha, meaning 'happy, prosperous land', was the welfare-state programme of U Nu's government in Burma, launched in 1952.</dd></div><div id="pythia"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/pythia">Pythia</a></dt><dd>The Pythia was the priestess of Apollo at Delphi who spoke the god's oracles.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-q"><h2>Q</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="qatabanian-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/qatabanian-language">Qatabanian language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Qatabanic)</span></dt><dd>Qatabanian is an Old South Arabian language spoken in the ancient kingdom of Qataban in Yemen and written in the South Arabian script.</dd></div><div id="qatar-and-dubai-riyal"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/qatar-and-dubai-riyal">Qatar and Dubai riyal</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Qatar-Dubai riyal)</span></dt><dd>The Qatar and Dubai riyal was the currency issued jointly by Qatar and Dubai from 1966 to 1973.</dd></div><div id="quarantine"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/quarantine">Quarantine</a></dt><dd>Quarantine is a restriction on the movement of people, animals and goods to prevent the spread of disease or pests; a 30-day isolation of newcomers, the trentino, was first imposed by the Republic of Ragusa (modern Dubrovnik, Croatia) in…</dd></div><div id="quechua"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Quechuan languages, Runa Simi)</span></dt><dd>Quechua is a family of languages spoken by some eight to ten million people in the Andes, chiefly in Peru, Bolivia and Ecuador.</dd></div><div id="quinine"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/quinine">Quinine</a></dt><dd>Quinine is an alkaloid from the bark of the cinchona tree, native to Peru, used to treat malaria; extracts of the bark were used against malaria from at least 1632, and the substance itself was first isolated in 1820 by the French…</dd></div><div id="quinquereme"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/quinquereme">Quinquereme</a></dt><dd>A quinquereme was a large ancient warship powered by oarsmen arranged in groups of five per vertical unit.</dd></div><div id="quipu"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/quipu">Quipu</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Khipu)</span></dt><dd>A quipu, or khipu, is a record-keeping device of knotted cords of cotton or camelid fibre, first undisputedly attested in the central Andes in the Middle Horizon, c. 600–1000 CE, when it was probably used by the Wari Empire.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-r"><h2>R</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="radio"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/radio">Radio</a> <span class="gl-aka">(wireless telegraphy)</span></dt><dd>Radio is communication by electromagnetic waves sent through the air; after Heinrich Hertz showed in experiments published between 1886 and 1888 that such waves could be transmitted, the Italian inventor Guglielmo Marconi was field…</dd></div><div id="radioactivity"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/radioactivity">Radioactivity</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Radioactive decay, Nuclear decay)</span></dt><dd>Radioactivity, or radioactive decay, is the process by which an unstable atomic nucleus loses energy by radiation, discovered in 1896 by Henri Becquerel when uranium salts blackened a photographic plate wrapped in black paper.</dd></div><div id="raffia-cloth"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/raffia-cloth">Raffia cloth</a></dt><dd>Raffia cloth is a textile woven from the fibres of the raffia palm, produced in Central Africa.</dd></div><div id="rajab"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/rajab">Rajab</a></dt><dd>Rajab is the seventh month of the Islamic lunar calendar.</dd></div><div id="rajah-of-sarawak"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/rajah-of-sarawak">Rajah of Sarawak</a> <span class="gl-aka">(White Rajah)</span></dt><dd>Rajah of Sarawak was the title of the Brooke family, the 'White Rajahs', who ruled Sarawak in northern Borneo from 1841 to 1946.</dd></div><div id="rajasuya"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/rajasuya">Rajasuya</a></dt><dd>The Rajasuya was a Vedic royal consecration ritual in which a king was anointed and proclaimed his supremacy over other rulers.</dd></div><div id="rajpramukh"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukh</a></dt><dd>Rajpramukh was the title of the head of a union of former princely states in the Republic of India between 1948 and 1956, an office comparable to a state governor.</dd></div><div id="ramlila"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ramlila">Ramlila</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Ram Lila)</span></dt><dd>Ramlila is a dramatic re-enactment of the life of the god Rama, based on the Ramayana and especially Tulsidas's Ramcharitmanas, performed across northern India in the weeks around the Dussehra festival.</dd></div><div id="rector-of-ragusa"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/rector-of-ragusa">Rector of Ragusa</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Rector of the Republic of Ragusa)</span></dt><dd>The Rector of Ragusa was the nominal head of state of the Republic of Ragusa (Dubrovnik) from 1358 to 1808.</dd></div><div id="refrigeration"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/refrigeration">Refrigeration</a></dt><dd>Refrigeration is the artificial cooling of a space, substance or system below the surrounding temperature; the harvesting of snow and ice is estimated to have begun before 1000 BCE, and artificial refrigeration began with a small machine…</dd></div><div id="reich-protector"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/reich-protector">Reich Protector</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Reichsprotektor)</span></dt><dd>Reich Protector, in German Reichsprotektor, was the title of the highest German official in the Protectorate of Bohemia and Moravia from 1939 to 1945, who held real power over the nominal Czech government.</dd></div><div id="reichskommissar"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/reichskommissar">Reichskommissar</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Reich Commissioner)</span></dt><dd>A Reichskommissar, or Reich Commissioner, was in Nazi Germany an official appointed to govern an occupied territory as a civil administrator answerable directly to Hitler.</dd></div><div id="reservoir"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">Reservoir</a> <span class="gl-aka">(reservoirs)</span></dt><dd>A reservoir is an artificial or enlarged body of water used to store water for irrigation, drinking or flood control.</dd></div><div id="rhinoplasty"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/rhinoplasty">Rhinoplasty</a></dt><dd>Rhinoplasty is surgery to reconstruct or reshape the nose.</dd></div><div id="riau-malay"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/riau-malay">Riau Malay</a></dt><dd>Riau Malay is the variety of Malay spoken in the Riau archipelago and the old Johor-Riau sultanate.</dd></div><div id="rice"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a></dt><dd>Rice is a cereal grain cultivated in flooded paddies or dry fields and is a staple food across much of Asia and parts of West Africa.</dd></div><div id="rice-terrace"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/rice-terrace">Rice terrace</a> <span class="gl-aka">(terraced paddies)</span></dt><dd>A rice terrace is a series of stepped, levelled fields cut into hillsides to allow the irrigated cultivation of rice on sloping ground.</dd></div><div id="riesling"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/riesling">Riesling</a></dt><dd>Riesling is a white grape variety native to the Rhine region of Germany, prized for aromatic, high-acid wines ranging from dry to sweet.</dd></div><div id="rocket"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/rocket">Rocket</a></dt><dd>A rocket is an elongated flying vehicle driven by a rocket engine, carrying all its propellant and so needing no surrounding air; gunpowder rockets evolved in China under the Song dynasty by the 13th century.</dd></div><div id="roman-concrete"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/roman-concrete">Roman concrete</a> <span class="gl-aka">(opus caementicium)</span></dt><dd>Roman concrete, or opus caementicium, is a building material used in ancient Rome: a hydraulic-setting cement mixed with an aggregate, in widespread use from about 150 BCE.</dd></div><div id="roman-dutch-law"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/roman-dutch-law">Roman-Dutch law</a></dt><dd>Roman-Dutch law is the legal system that developed in the Netherlands in the sixteenth to eighteenth centuries by combining Roman civil law with Dutch custom, and was described by jurists such as Hugo Grotius.</dd></div><div id="romansh"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/romansh">Romansh</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Romansh language)</span></dt><dd>Romansh is a Romance language spoken in the canton of Graubünden in eastern Switzerland, descended from the Latin of the Roman province of Raetia.</dd></div><div id="romny-culture"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/romny-culture">Romny culture</a></dt><dd>The Romny culture was an archaeological culture of the 8th to 10th centuries on the left bank of the Dnieper in present-day Ukraine and Russia.</dd></div><div id="rubber"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/rubber">Rubber</a></dt><dd>Rubber is an elastic material produced from the latex of certain tropical trees, which became an important industrial commodity in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries with the growth of bicycles, automobiles and electrical…</dd></div><div id="rupee"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/rupee">Rupee</a></dt><dd>The rupee is a currency unit of the Indian subcontinent, descended from the silver rupiya coin introduced in the sixteenth century and later standardised under Mughal rule.</dd></div><div id="rupiya"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/rupiya">Rupiya</a></dt><dd>The rupiya was a silver coin introduced in northern India in the sixteenth century under Sher Shah Suri and adopted as the standard silver coinage of the Mughal Empire.</dd></div><div id="russification"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a></dt><dd>Russification was the policy of spreading the Russian language, culture and administration among the non-Russian peoples of the Russian Empire and later the Soviet Union.</dd></div><div id="rye"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/rye">Rye</a></dt><dd>Rye is a hardy cereal grain that grows well in cool climates and poorer soils, long a staple of northern and eastern European agriculture.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-s"><h2>S</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="sadeh"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sadeh">Sadeh</a></dt><dd>Sadeh is an ancient Iranian festival celebrated with great bonfires fifty days before Nowruz, at the end of January, to honour fire and mark the waning of winter.</dd></div><div id="sail"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sail">Sail</a></dt><dd>A sail is a tensile structure of fabric or another membrane that uses the wind to propel a craft; sailing boats appear on Cucuteni–Trypillian ceramics from the 6th millennium BCE, and excavations of the Ubaid period in Mesopotamia give…</dd></div><div id="saint-lucian-creole"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/saint-lucian-creole">Saint Lucian Creole</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Kwéyòl)</span></dt><dd>Saint Lucian Creole, or Kwéyòl, is a French-based creole language spoken by most people in Saint Lucia.</dd></div><div id="saka-era"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/saka-era">Saka era</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Shaka era)</span></dt><dd>The Saka era is a calendar era beginning in 78 CE, used widely in India and in Hindu-Buddhist Java and Bali to date inscriptions and chronicles.</dd></div><div id="salinisation"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/salinisation">Salinisation</a></dt><dd>Salinisation is the build-up of salts in soil, often caused by long-term irrigation in hot, dry climates where evaporation leaves mineral deposits behind.</dd></div><div id="salt-trade"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/salt-trade">Salt trade</a></dt><dd>The salt trade refers to the long-distance exchange of salt, a vital commodity for human diet and food preservation.</dd></div><div id="san-pedro-cactus"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/san-pedro-cactus">San Pedro cactus</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Echinopsis pachanoi)</span></dt><dd>The San Pedro cactus, Echinopsis pachanoi, is a fast-growing columnar cactus native to the Andes of Peru and Ecuador.</dd></div><div id="sancai"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sancai">Sancai</a></dt><dd>Sancai, meaning three colours, is a type of Chinese lead-glazed pottery decorated mainly in amber, green and cream, which flourished under the Tang Dynasty.</dd></div><div id="sanctions"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">Sanctions</a></dt><dd>Sanctions are penalties, usually economic, imposed by states or international bodies to pressure another state to change its behaviour.</dd></div><div id="sangpyeong-tongbo"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sangpyeong-tongbo">Sangpyeong tongbo</a></dt><dd>The Sangpyeong tongbo was a copper coin of Joseon Korea, cast in large numbers from 1678 under King Sukjong as the country's standard currency.</dd></div><div id="sanskrit"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a></dt><dd>Sanskrit is a classical Indo-Aryan language of South Asia, used for religious, literary, philosophical and scientific texts over more than three millennia.</dd></div><div id="sapa-inca"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a></dt><dd>Sapa Inca, the 'unique Inca', was the title of the ruler of the Inca state.</dd></div><div id="sarissa"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sarissa">Sarissa</a></dt><dd>The sarissa was a very long pike, several metres in length, carried by the infantry of the Macedonian phalanx.</dd></div><div id="savannah"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a></dt><dd>Savannah is a type of tropical grassland with scattered trees, found widely across Africa between the rainforest and the desert.</dd></div><div id="secretary-general-of-the-united-nations"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/secretary-general-of-the-united-nations">Secretary-General of the United Nations</a></dt><dd>The Secretary-General of the United Nations is the chief administrative officer of the United Nations and head of its Secretariat, appointed by the General Assembly on the recommendation of the Security Council, usually for five-year terms.</dd></div><div id="seed-drill"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/seed-drill">Seed drill</a></dt><dd>A seed drill is a device that sows seeds by placing them in the soil at a set depth; the Babylonians used primitive seed drills around 1400 BCE.</dd></div><div id="semitic-languages"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Semitic)</span></dt><dd>The Semitic languages are a family of languages of the Near East and North Africa that includes Akkadian, Aramaic, Hebrew, Phoenician, Arabic and Ge'ez.</dd></div><div id="serfdom"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">Serfdom</a></dt><dd>Serfdom was a form of unfree labour in which peasants were bound to the land and owed labour or dues to a lord.</dd></div><div id="sericulture"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sericulture">Sericulture</a></dt><dd>Sericulture is the raising of silkworms for the production of raw silk, typically by feeding them on mulberry leaves.</dd></div><div id="sesame"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sesame">Sesame</a></dt><dd>Sesame is a flowering plant cultivated for its oil-rich seeds, one of the oldest oilseed crops.</dd></div><div id="seven-wonders-of-the-ancient-world"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/seven-wonders-of-the-ancient-world">Seven Wonders of the Ancient World</a></dt><dd>The Seven Wonders of the Ancient World are a list of remarkable monuments drawn up by Greek writers of the Hellenistic period: the Great Pyramid of Giza, the Hanging Gardens of Babylon, the statue of Zeus at Olympia, the Temple of Artemis…</dd></div><div id="sexagesimal"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sexagesimal">Sexagesimal</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Base 60)</span></dt><dd>Sexagesimal, or base 60, is a numeral system with sixty as its base, which originated with the Sumerians in the 3rd millennium BCE and passed to the Babylonians.</dd></div><div id="sharif-of-mecca"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sharif-of-mecca">Sharif of Mecca</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Sharifate of Mecca, Emir of Mecca)</span></dt><dd>The Sharif of Mecca was the ruler of Mecca and much of the Hejaz, chosen from among the descendants of the Prophet Muhammad through his grandson Hasan, from the 10th century until 1925.</dd></div><div id="shawl"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/shawl">Shawl</a> <span class="gl-aka">(shawls, pashmina)</span></dt><dd>A shawl is a large piece of cloth worn over the shoulders or head.</dd></div><div id="shehu-of-borno"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/shehu-of-borno">Shehu of Borno</a></dt><dd>Shehu of Borno is the title of the rulers of Borno in north-eastern Nigeria from the al-Kanemi dynasty, founded by the scholar Muhammad al-Amin al-Kanemi in the early 19th century.</dd></div><div id="shimaore"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/shimaore">Shimaore</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Mahorais)</span></dt><dd>Shimaore is a Bantu language spoken in Mayotte, one of the varieties of Comorian and closely related to Swahili.</dd></div><div id="shina-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/shina-language">Shina language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Shina)</span></dt><dd>Shina is a Dardic Indo-Aryan language spoken in Gilgit-Baltistan in northern Pakistan and in neighbouring parts of Kashmir and Kohistan.</dd></div><div id="shirvanshah"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a></dt><dd>Shirvanshah, &quot;king of Shirvan&quot;, was the title of the rulers of Shirvan in the eastern Caucasus.</dd></div><div id="shudra"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudra</a></dt><dd>Shudra is the fourth of the four varnas, the classes of traditional Hindu society described in Vedic and later Sanskrit texts, below the Brahmins, Kshatriyas and Vaishyas.</dd></div><div id="siculo-arabic"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/siculo-arabic">Siculo-Arabic</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Sicilian Arabic)</span></dt><dd>Siculo-Arabic was the variety of Arabic spoken in Sicily and Malta from the Islamic conquest in the 9th century.</dd></div><div id="sidetic-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sidetic-language">Sidetic language</a></dt><dd>Sidetic was an Anatolian language spoken at Side on the Pamphylian coast of southern Turkey, related to Luwian and Lycian.</dd></div><div id="siege-engine"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine">Siege engine</a> <span class="gl-aka">(siege engines)</span></dt><dd>A siege engine is a device designed to break or bypass fortifications, such as a battering ram, siege tower, catapult or trebuchet.</dd></div><div id="siege-tower"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-tower">Siege tower</a> <span class="gl-aka">(siege towers)</span></dt><dd>A siege tower is a mobile wooden tower used to bring attackers to the top of fortification walls.</dd></div><div id="siege-warfare"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-warfare">Siege warfare</a></dt><dd>Siege warfare is the conduct of military operations to capture or defend fortified places, through blockade, assault, mining or bombardment.</dd></div><div id="silk"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/silk">Silk</a></dt><dd>Silk is a fine, strong fabric made from the cocoons of silkworms, first produced in China.</dd></div><div id="silk-road"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a></dt><dd>The Silk Road was the network of overland trade routes linking China with Central Asia, Iran, the Middle East and the Mediterranean.</dd></div><div id="silver"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a></dt><dd>Silver is a precious metal used throughout history for coinage, jewellery and as a store of value.</dd></div><div id="sinhala-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sinhala-language">Sinhala language</a></dt><dd>Sinhala is the Indo-Aryan language of the Sinhalese people and one of the official languages of Sri Lanka, written in its own script.</dd></div><div id="skat"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/skat">Skat</a></dt><dd>Skat is a three-player trick-taking card game developed in Altenburg in Thuringia around 1810–1820, drawing on older games such as Schafkopf and Tarock.</dd></div><div id="skyscraper"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/skyscraper">Skyscraper</a></dt><dd>A skyscraper is a very tall building, typically defined as at least 150 metres high, of a type that first emerged in the United States in the late 19th century; many regard the ten-storey Home Insurance Building in Chicago, built in…</dd></div><div id="slave-raiding"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/slave-raiding">Slave raiding</a></dt><dd>Slave raiding is the capture of people by force to be enslaved and sold or used for labour.</dd></div><div id="slavery"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> <span class="gl-aka">(enslaved)</span></dt><dd>Slavery is the condition in which people are treated as property and forced to work.</dd></div><div id="sling"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sling">Sling</a></dt><dd>The sling is a simple projectile weapon consisting of a cord and pouch used to hurl stones or lead shot.</dd></div><div id="smallpox"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">Smallpox</a></dt><dd>Smallpox is a highly contagious viral disease that was a leading cause of death in many societies until its eradication in 1980.</dd></div><div id="smallpox-vaccine"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/smallpox-vaccine">Smallpox vaccine</a></dt><dd>The smallpox vaccine is the first vaccine developed against a contagious disease: in 1796 the British physician Edward Jenner showed that infection with the mild cowpox virus gave immunity to smallpox; his Inquiry of 1798 presented cowpox…</dd></div><div id="soap"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/soap">Soap</a></dt><dd>Soap is a salt of a fatty acid, often made by mixing fats or oils with an alkali, used for washing and cleaning; the earliest recorded evidence of soap-like materials comes from Mesopotamia, c. 2800 BCE.</dd></div><div id="soil-erosion"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/soil-erosion">Soil erosion</a></dt><dd>Soil erosion is the loss of the fertile top layer of soil through the action of water, wind or overuse.</dd></div><div id="solar-cell"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/solar-cell">Solar cell</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Photovoltaic cell, PV cell)</span></dt><dd>A solar cell, or photovoltaic cell, is an electronic device that converts the energy of light directly into electricity through the photovoltaic effect; the French physicist Edmond Becquerel built the first in 1839.</dd></div><div id="solidus"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/solidus">Solidus</a> <span class="gl-aka">(solidi)</span></dt><dd>The solidus was a gold coin introduced by the Roman emperor Constantine in the early fourth century.</dd></div><div id="son-of-heaven"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/son-of-heaven">Son of Heaven</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Tianzi)</span></dt><dd>Son of Heaven (tianzi) was a title of the Chinese ruler, first used by the kings of the Western Zhou, expressing the idea that the ruler held a mandate from Heaven to govern all under Heaven.</dd></div><div id="soqotri-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/soqotri-language">Soqotri language</a></dt><dd>Soqotri is a Modern South Arabian language spoken on Socotra and its neighbouring islands, part of Yemen.</dd></div><div id="sorghum"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">Sorghum</a></dt><dd>Sorghum is a drought-tolerant cereal grain that was domesticated in Africa and became a staple across the savannah and Sahel.</dd></div><div id="sosloviye"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sosloviye">Sosloviye</a> <span class="gl-aka">(soslovie)</span></dt><dd>Sosloviye was the term for the legal estates of the Russian Empire, introduced in the 18th and 19th centuries to approximate Europe's estates of the realm.</dd></div><div id="south-picene-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/south-picene-language">South Picene language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(South Picene)</span></dt><dd>South Picene is an extinct Sabellic language of the Italic family, known from about two dozen inscriptions of the 6th to 4th centuries BCE found in the Marche and Abruzzo, Italy.</dd></div><div id="sovereign-default"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sovereign-default">Sovereign default</a> <span class="gl-aka">(bankruptcies)</span></dt><dd>A sovereign default is a failure by a government to repay its debts.</dd></div><div id="spanish-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/spanish-language">Spanish language</a></dt><dd>The Spanish language is a Romance language that originated in Castile.</dd></div><div id="special-administrative-regions-of-china"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/special-administrative-regions-of-china">Special administrative regions of China</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Special Administrative Region)</span></dt><dd>The special administrative regions of China are Hong Kong and Macau, created under the principle of 'one country, two systems' when Britain returned Hong Kong in 1997 and Portugal returned Macau in 1999.</dd></div><div id="spice-trade"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">Spice trade</a> <span class="gl-aka">(spice monopolies, spices)</span></dt><dd>The spice trade was the long-distance commerce in spices such as pepper, cinnamon, cloves and nutmeg from South and Southeast Asia.</dd></div><div id="spinning-jenny"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/spinning-jenny">Spinning jenny</a></dt><dd>The spinning jenny is a multi-spindle spinning frame invented in 1764–1765 by James Hargreaves at Stanhill, Oswaldtwistle, in Lancashire, England.</dd></div><div id="spinning-wheel"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/spinning-wheel">Spinning wheel</a></dt><dd>The spinning wheel is a device in which a hand- or foot-turned wheel drives the spindle that spins fibres into thread or yarn; its origin is disputed, and some scholars hold that Chinese dictionaries of the 2nd century CE mention it.</dd></div><div id="spirit-medium"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/spirit-medium">Spirit medium</a> <span class="gl-aka">(spirit mediums)</span></dt><dd>A spirit medium is a person believed to be possessed by and speak for ancestral or other spirits.</dd></div><div id="sranan-tongo"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sranan-tongo">Sranan Tongo</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Sranan)</span></dt><dd>Sranan Tongo is an English-based creole language that arose among enslaved Africans in Suriname in the seventeenth century, during the English period of the colony, and later absorbed many Dutch and Portuguese words.</dd></div><div id="standard-chinese"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/standard-chinese">Standard Chinese</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Standard Mandarin, Putonghua)</span></dt><dd>Standard Chinese, or Standard Mandarin, is the official language of the People's Republic of China, based on the pronunciation of Beijing and the grammar of modern vernacular Mandarin literature.</dd></div><div id="star-catalogue"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/star-catalogue">Star catalogue</a> <span class="gl-aka">(star catalogues)</span></dt><dd>A star catalogue is a list of stars with their positions and brightness.</dd></div><div id="state-counsellor-of-myanmar"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/state-counsellor-of-myanmar">State Counsellor of Myanmar</a></dt><dd>The State Counsellor of Myanmar was a post created in April 2016 for Aung San Suu Kyi, who was barred from the presidency by the 2008 constitution because her sons held foreign citizenship.</dd></div><div id="state-elder"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/state-elder">State Elder</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Riigivanem)</span></dt><dd>The State Elder (Riigivanem) was the head of government of interwar Estonia, who under the constitution of 1920 also acted as head of state, as there was no separate presidency.</dd></div><div id="states-of-germany"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/states-of-germany">States of Germany</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Länder, Bundesländer)</span></dt><dd>The states of Germany, or Länder, are the sixteen federated states of the Federal Republic of Germany, each with its own constitution, parliament and government.</dd></div><div id="stato-da-mar"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/stato-da-mar">Stato da Mar</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Stato da Màr)</span></dt><dd>The Stato da Mar ('State of the Sea') was the collective name for the overseas possessions of the Republic of Venice, as distinct from its Italian mainland territories, the Domini di Terraferma.</dd></div><div id="steam-engine"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">Steam engine</a></dt><dd>A steam engine is a heat engine that does mechanical work with steam, usually by pushing a piston in a cylinder; the Spanish inventor Jerónimo de Ayanz patented the first steam-powered water pump, for draining mines, in 1606.</dd></div><div id="steam-locomotive"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/steam-locomotive">Steam locomotive</a></dt><dd>A steam locomotive is a self-propelled steam engine on wheels that moves itself and other vehicles by expanding steam; it was first developed in the United Kingdom, where on 21 February 1804 one of Richard Trevithick's locomotives made…</dd></div><div id="steam-turbine"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/steam-turbine">Steam turbine</a></dt><dd>A steam turbine is a heat engine that extracts energy from pressurised steam to turn a rotating shaft; Taqi al-Din described one that turned a spit in Ottoman Egypt in 1551, after the aeolipile of Hero of Alexandria.</dd></div><div id="steamship"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/steamship">Steamship</a> <span class="gl-aka">(steam-powered warships)</span></dt><dd>A steamship is a ship powered by a steam engine.</dd></div><div id="steel"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/steel">Steel</a></dt><dd>Steel is an alloy of iron and carbon, much stronger than iron.</dd></div><div id="stepwell"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/stepwell">Stepwell</a> <span class="gl-aka">(vav, baori)</span></dt><dd>A stepwell, or vav, is a well, cistern or pond reached by a long corridor of steps down to the water, developed in India, where the earliest archaeological evidence is at Dholavira.</dd></div><div id="sternpost-rudder"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sternpost-rudder">Sternpost rudder</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Sternpost-mounted rudder, Stern-mounted rudder)</span></dt><dd>A sternpost rudder is a steering blade hung at the centre of a ship's stern, first known from pottery models of Chinese junks of the 1st century CE, under the Han dynasty.</dd></div><div id="stirrup"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/stirrup">Stirrup</a></dt><dd>A stirrup is a light frame or ring hung by a strap from a saddle to hold a rider's foot, first known in China under the Jin dynasty in the early 4th century CE.</dd></div><div id="stone-tools"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">Stone tools</a></dt><dd>Stone tools are implements shaped from stone by knapping or grinding; the oldest known, from Lomekwi near Lake Turkana in Kenya, are dated to 3.3 million years ago, about a million years before the genus Homo.</dd></div><div id="submarine"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/submarine">Submarine</a></dt><dd>A submarine is a watercraft capable of independent operation underwater; the first submersible of which reliable information exists, propelled by oars, was built in 1620 by Cornelis Drebbel, a Dutchman in the service of James I of England.</dd></div><div id="subsidy"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">Subsidy</a> <span class="gl-aka">(subsidies)</span></dt><dd>A subsidy is a payment made by a state to support another party.</dd></div><div id="sugar-plantation"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar plantation</a> <span class="gl-aka">(sugar-plantation, sugar)</span></dt><dd>A sugar plantation is a large agricultural estate growing sugarcane for sugar production, typically using intensive labour.</dd></div><div id="sugarcane"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">Sugarcane</a></dt><dd>Sugarcane is a tall tropical grass whose stalks are crushed to produce sugar.</dd></div><div id="sumerian-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-language">Sumerian language</a></dt><dd>The Sumerian language was the language of ancient Sumer, one of the earliest written languages.</dd></div><div id="sumptuary-law"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sumptuary-law">Sumptuary law</a> <span class="gl-aka">(sumptuary)</span></dt><dd>A sumptuary law is a law that restricts consumption, such as clothing or luxury goods, often according to social rank.</dd></div><div id="supreme-commander-for-the-allied-powers"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/supreme-commander-for-the-allied-powers">Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers</a> <span class="gl-aka">(SCAP)</span></dt><dd>The Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers (SCAP) was the title of the head of the Allied occupation of Japan, held by General Douglas MacArthur from 1945 and by Matthew Ridgway from 1951, and the name of his headquarters in Tokyo.</dd></div><div id="supreme-leader-of-iran"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/supreme-leader-of-iran">Supreme Leader of Iran</a></dt><dd>The Supreme Leader of Iran is the highest office of the Islamic Republic of Iran, created by the constitution of 1979 on the principle of the guardianship of the Islamic jurist.</dd></div><div id="swargadeo"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/swargadeo">Swargadeo</a></dt><dd>Swargadeo, &quot;lord of heaven&quot;, was the title of the kings of the Ahom kingdom of Assam, rendering the Tai Ahom title Chao-pha.</dd></div><div id="swazi-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/swazi-language">Swazi language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(siSwati)</span></dt><dd>Swazi, or siSwati, is a Bantu language of the Nguni group, closely related to Zulu.</dd></div><div id="sweet-potato"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/sweet-potato">Sweet potato</a> <span class="gl-aka">(sweet potatoes)</span></dt><dd>The sweet potato is a starchy root crop native to the Americas that spread across the world after the sixteenth century through Spanish and Portuguese trade networks.</dd></div><div id="syennesis"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/syennesis">Syennesis</a></dt><dd>Syennesis was the name, or perhaps the title, of the native kings of Cilicia in southern Anatolia during the period of Achaemenid rule.</dd></div><div id="syiem"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/syiem">Syiem</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Siem)</span></dt><dd>Syiem is the title of the chief of a traditional Khasi state, or hima, in the hills of present-day Meghalaya, India.</dd></div><div id="synthetic-dye"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/synthetic-dye">Synthetic dye</a> <span class="gl-aka">(dyestuff)</span></dt><dd>Synthetic dyes are colourants produced by industrial chemistry rather than extracted from plants or animals, a technology that developed rapidly from the mid-nineteenth century.</dd></div><div id="syriac"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a></dt><dd>Syriac is a dialect of Aramaic that became the main literary and liturgical language of many Christian communities in the Near East from the early centuries CE.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-t"><h2>T</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="tagalog-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalog language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Tagalog, Filipino language)</span></dt><dd>Tagalog is an Austronesian language of the central Philippines, spoken natively around Manila and in southern Luzon.</dd></div><div id="tai-tham-script"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tai-tham-script">Tai Tham script</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Lanna script)</span></dt><dd>The Tai Tham script, also called the Lanna script, is a writing system derived from the Mon script and used since about the 14th century for Northern Thai, Tai Lue, Tai Khün and Lao religious texts.</dd></div><div id="talent"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/talent">Talent</a> <span class="gl-aka">(talents)</span></dt><dd>The talent was a large unit of weight used in the ancient Mediterranean and Near East, commonly used to measure quantities of silver and gold for payments.</dd></div><div id="talmudic-academy"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/talmudic-academy">Talmudic academy</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Talmudic)</span></dt><dd>A Talmudic academy, or yeshiva, is an institution for the advanced study of the Talmud and Jewish law.</dd></div><div id="tamil-brahmi"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tamil-brahmi">Tamil-Brahmi</a></dt><dd>Tamil-Brahmi is a variant of the Brahmi script used to write the Tamil language from around the 3rd or 2nd century BCE.</dd></div><div id="tank"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tank">Tank</a> <span class="gl-aka">(landship)</span></dt><dd>A tank is an armoured fighting vehicle combining heavy firepower, strong armour and the mobility of tracks and a powerful engine; the first British prototype, Little Willie, was built at William Foster &amp; Co. in Lincoln, England, in 1915.</dd></div><div id="tanka"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tanka">Tanka</a></dt><dd>The tanka was a silver coin introduced as standard currency in the Delhi Sultanate by Iltutmish in the early thirteenth century.</dd></div><div id="taoiseach"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/taoiseach">Taoiseach</a></dt><dd>The Taoiseach is the head of government of Ireland, nominated by Dáil Éireann and appointed by the President.</dd></div><div id="tatich"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tatich">Tatich</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Patron of the Cross)</span></dt><dd>Tatich was the title of the supreme leader of the Cruzob, the rebel Maya of Chan Santa Cruz during the Caste War of Yucatán.</dd></div><div id="tea"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tea">Tea</a></dt><dd>Tea is a beverage made from the leaves of the tea plant, native to southern China.</dd></div><div id="telegraph"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">Telegraph</a></dt><dd>The telegraph was a system for transmitting messages over long distances by electrical signals along wires, developed in the nineteenth century.</dd></div><div id="telephone"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/telephone">Telephone</a></dt><dd>The telephone is a device that turns the voice into electrical signals and back into sound at a distance; Johann Philipp Reis demonstrated the first device to transmit a voice by electronic signals before the Physical Society of Frankfurt…</dd></div><div id="telescope"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/telescope">Telescope</a></dt><dd>A telescope is an instrument for observing distant objects by the radiation they emit, absorb or reflect; the first known practical telescopes, refractors with glass lenses, were invented in the Netherlands at the beginning of the 17th…</dd></div><div id="television"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/television">Television</a> <span class="gl-aka">(TV)</span></dt><dd>Television is a medium for transmitting moving images and sound; the first demonstration of the live transmission of images was given by Georges Rignoux and A. Fournier in Paris in 1909, with a matrix of 64 selenium cells.</dd></div><div id="telugu-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/telugu-language">Telugu language</a></dt><dd>Telugu is a Dravidian language of south-eastern India, the main language of Andhra Pradesh and Telangana and one of the most widely spoken languages of India.</dd></div><div id="tenancy"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tenancy">Tenancy</a></dt><dd>Tenancy is an arrangement in which cultivators farm land owned by others in return for rent, labour or a share of the harvest.</dd></div><div id="terp"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/terp">Terp</a></dt><dd>A terp is an artificial dwelling mound built in the coastal salt marshes of the northern Netherlands and north-western Germany, where it is called a Wurt, to keep homes and livestock above storm floods.</dd></div><div id="tetum-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tetum-language">Tetum language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Tetun)</span></dt><dd>Tetum is an Austronesian language of the island of Timor and, with Portuguese, one of the two official languages of Timor-Leste.</dd></div><div id="theatre"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a></dt><dd>Theatre is the performance of drama before an audience, and also the buildings designed for it.</dd></div><div id="thewa"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/thewa">Thewa</a></dt><dd>Thewa is a jewellery technique from Pratapgarh in Rajasthan, India, in which finely worked sheet gold is fused onto coloured glass.</dd></div><div id="thin-orange-ware"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/thin-orange-ware">Thin Orange ware</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Thin Orange)</span></dt><dd>Thin Orange ware is a fine, thin-walled orange pottery produced in southern Puebla, in central Mexico, during the Classic period, roughly the third to the sixth centuries CE.</dd></div><div id="thrall"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/thrall">Thrall</a> <span class="gl-aka">(þræll)</span></dt><dd>A thrall was a slave or serf in Viking Age Scandinavia, the lowest of the three orders of Norse society, below the freeman (karl) and the nobleman (jarl).</dd></div><div id="three-field-system"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/three-field-system">Three-field system</a></dt><dd>The three-field system is a crop rotation in which a field bears one set of crops in the first year, another in the second, and lies fallow in the third; it was first used in China in the Eastern Zhou period.</dd></div><div id="tibeto-burman-languages"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tibeto-burman-languages">Tibeto-Burman languages</a></dt><dd>Tibeto-Burman is the name for the large group of Sino-Tibetan languages other than Chinese, spoken from the Himalayas to Myanmar and south-western China.</dd></div><div id="tigrinya-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tigrinya-language">Tigrinya language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Tigrinya)</span></dt><dd>Tigrinya is a Semitic language of the Horn of Africa, spoken in the highlands of Eritrea and in the Tigray region of Ethiopia.</dd></div><div id="timber"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/timber">Timber</a></dt><dd>Timber is wood used for construction, shipbuilding and fuel, and was a strategic resource for many pre-modern states.</dd></div><div id="tin"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tin">Tin</a></dt><dd>Tin is a metal that, when alloyed with copper, produces bronze, making it a strategic commodity of the ancient world.</dd></div><div id="tisha-bav"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tisha-bav">Tisha B'Av</a></dt><dd>Tisha B'Av, the ninth day of the Hebrew month of Av, is the annual fast day in Judaism that mourns the destruction of the First Temple by the Babylonians in 587 or 586 BCE and of the Second Temple by the Romans in 70 CE.</dd></div><div id="tok-pisin"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tok-pisin">Tok Pisin</a></dt><dd>Tok Pisin is an English-based creole language spoken across Papua New Guinea and one of its official languages.</dd></div><div id="tomato"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tomato">Tomato</a></dt><dd>The tomato is a fruit-bearing plant native to the Americas that spread to Europe and the rest of the world after the Spanish conquests of the sixteenth century.</dd></div><div id="torrens-title"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/torrens-title">Torrens title</a></dt><dd>Torrens title is a system of land registration in which the state keeps a central register of land ownership and guarantees the title of the registered owner.</dd></div><div id="total-war"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/total-war">Total war</a></dt><dd>Total war is warfare in which states mobilise their entire population, economy and resources, and in which the distinction between combatants and civilians is eroded.</dd></div><div id="tower-of-skulls"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tower-of-skulls">Tower of skulls</a> <span class="gl-aka">(towers of skulls)</span></dt><dd>A tower of skulls was a structure built from the heads of the slain, raised as a deliberate display of terror after the capture of a city.</dd></div><div id="trans-saharan-trade"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/trans-saharan-trade">Trans-Saharan trade</a> <span class="gl-aka">(trans-Saharan)</span></dt><dd>The trans-Saharan trade was the network of caravan routes across the Sahara Desert linking North Africa and the Mediterranean with West and Central Africa.</dd></div><div id="transhumance"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/transhumance">Transhumance</a></dt><dd>Transhumance is the seasonal movement of livestock and herders between different pastures, typically between lowland winter grazing and highland summer grazing.</dd></div><div id="transistor"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/transistor">Transistor</a></dt><dd>A transistor is a semiconductor device that amplifies or switches electrical signals and power; the first working one, a point-contact transistor, was invented in 1947 by John Bardeen, Walter Brattain and William Shockley at Bell Labs in…</dd></div><div id="trebuchet"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/trebuchet">Trebuchet</a></dt><dd>The trebuchet is a siege engine that uses a pivoting arm to hurl heavy projectiles, either by human traction or by a heavy counterweight.</dd></div><div id="trepanning"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/trepanning">Trepanning</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Trepanation, Trephination)</span></dt><dd>Trepanning, or trepanation, is a surgical intervention in which a hole is drilled or scraped into the human skull, perhaps the oldest surgical procedure with archaeological evidence; at a burial site in France dated to 6500 BCE, 40 of 120…</dd></div><div id="triangular-trade"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/triangular-trade">Triangular trade</a> <span class="gl-aka">(triangular commerce)</span></dt><dd>The triangular trade was the Atlantic commercial system linking Europe, West Africa and the Americas from the sixteenth to the nineteenth centuries.</dd></div><div id="trireme"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/trireme">Trireme</a> <span class="gl-aka">(trieres)</span></dt><dd>The trireme was an ancient Mediterranean galley driven by three banks of oars with one man to each oar, first referred to at the end of the 8th century BCE; scholars are divided on whether it came from Greece or Phoenicia.</dd></div><div id="tsetse-fly"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tsetse-fly">Tsetse fly</a> <span class="gl-aka">(tsetse-fly)</span></dt><dd>The tsetse fly is a blood-feeding insect of tropical Africa that transmits trypanosomiasis, a disease fatal to horses and cattle.</dd></div><div id="tubba"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tubba">Tubba'</a></dt><dd>Tubba' was the name by which Arab and Islamic tradition remembered the kings of Himyar in ancient Yemen.</dd></div><div id="tui-lau"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tui-lau">Tui Lau</a></dt><dd>Tui Lau is a Fijian chiefly title meaning paramount chief of the Lau Islands in eastern Fiji.</dd></div><div id="tui-viti"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tui-viti">Tui Viti</a></dt><dd>Tui Viti, meaning 'king of Fiji', is the title claimed in the mid-19th century by Seru Epenisa Cakobau, the paramount chief of Bau.</dd></div><div id="tulughma"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tulughma">Tulughma</a></dt><dd>Tulughma was a flanking tactic of Central Asian origin in which mobile cavalry units swept around the enemy's flanks to attack them from the sides and rear.</dd></div><div id="tumbuka-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tumbuka-language">Tumbuka language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Chitumbuka)</span></dt><dd>Tumbuka, or Chitumbuka, is a Bantu language spoken in northern Malawi and eastern Zambia.</dd></div><div id="tumshuqese-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tumshuqese-language">Tumshuqese language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Tumshuqese)</span></dt><dd>Tumshuqese is an extinct Eastern Iranian language of the Saka group, known from documents of about the 7th to 10th centuries found at Tumshuq on the northern edge of the Tarim Basin, in Xinjiang.</dd></div><div id="tupi"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tupi">Tupi</a></dt><dd>Tupi is an indigenous language of Brazil, spoken by the Tupi peoples of the Atlantic coast at the time of Portuguese colonisation.</dd></div><div id="turtle-ship"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/turtle-ship">Turtle ship</a> <span class="gl-aka">(geobukseon, gwiseon)</span></dt><dd>The turtle ship (geobukseon) was a Korean warship named after its covering, said to resemble a turtle's shell, and first recorded in 1413 in the Annals of the Joseon Dynasty.</dd></div><div id="tui-haatakalaua"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tui-haatakalaua">Tuʻi Haʻatakalaua</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Tu'i Ha'atakalaua)</span></dt><dd>The Tuʻi Haʻatakalaua was the second line of Tongan kings, created in the 15th century after the assassination of the Tuʻi Tonga Takalaua.</dd></div><div id="tui-kanokupolu"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tui-kanokupolu">Tuʻi Kanokupolu</a></dt><dd>Tuʻi Kanokupolu is the title of the third and youngest of Tonga's royal lines, founded in the early 17th century by a son of the Tuʻi Haʻatakalaua.</dd></div><div id="typhus"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/typhus">Typhus</a></dt><dd>Typhus is a group of bacterial infectious diseases, often spread by lice, that cause fever and can be highly fatal in crowded or wartime conditions.</dd></div><div id="tyrian-purple"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/tyrian-purple">Tyrian purple</a> <span class="gl-aka">(purple-dyed, murex)</span></dt><dd>Tyrian purple is a deep purple dye produced from murex sea snails, named after the Phoenician city of Tyre.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-u"><h2>U</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="u-boat"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/u-boat">U-boat</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Unterseeboot)</span></dt><dd>U-boat is the English name for German military submarines, from the German Unterseeboot.</dd></div><div id="ukiyo-e"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ukiyo-e">Ukiyo-e</a></dt><dd>Ukiyo-e, meaning 'pictures of the floating world', is a genre of Japanese woodblock prints and paintings that flourished from the seventeenth to the nineteenth centuries.</dd></div><div id="umm-an-nar-culture"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/umm-an-nar-culture">Umm an-Nar culture</a></dt><dd>The Umm an-Nar culture was a Bronze Age culture of south-eastern Arabia, in modern Oman and the United Arab Emirates, dated about 2600–2000 BCE and named after an island off Abu Dhabi.</dd></div><div id="undang"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/undang">Undang</a></dt><dd>The Undang are the four great territorial chiefs of Negeri Sembilan in Malaysia, ruling the districts of Sungai Ujong, Jelebu, Johol and Rembau.</dd></div><div id="undeutsche"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/undeutsche">Undeutsche</a></dt><dd>Undeutsche (&quot;non-Germans&quot;) was the term used in medieval and early modern Livonia for the native Estonian and Latvian population, as opposed to the German-speaking nobility, clergy and burghers.</dd></div><div id="union-flag"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/union-flag">Union Flag</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Union Jack)</span></dt><dd>The Union Flag, or Union Jack, is the national flag of the United Kingdom.</dd></div><div id="universal-suffrage"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/universal-suffrage">Universal suffrage</a> <span class="gl-aka">(universal male suffrage)</span></dt><dd>Universal suffrage is the right of all adult citizens to vote, regardless of property, class or, in its full form, sex.</dd></div><div id="urdu"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a></dt><dd>Urdu is an Indo-Aryan language closely related to Hindi, written in a Perso-Arabic script and rich in Persian and Arabic vocabulary.</dd></div><div id="uruk-period"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/uruk-period">Uruk period</a></dt><dd>The Uruk period is the archaeological period of Mesopotamia from about 4000 to 3100 BCE, named after the city of Uruk.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-v"><h2>V</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="v-weapons"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/v-weapons">V-weapons</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Vergeltungswaffen, Vengeance weapons)</span></dt><dd>The V-weapons were long-range weapons used by Nazi Germany in the last year of the Second World War: the V-1 flying bomb and the V-2 ballistic missile, the first of its kind.</dd></div><div id="variolation"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/variolation">Variolation</a> <span class="gl-aka">(smallpox inoculation)</span></dt><dd>Variolation was the deliberate infection of a person with material from a smallpox patient, usually rubbed into scratches in the skin or, in China, blown up the nose as powdered scabs, to cause a mild illness that gave immunity; its…</dd></div><div id="venetic"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/venetic">Venetic</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Venetic language)</span></dt><dd>Venetic is an extinct Indo-European language spoken by the ancient Veneti in north-eastern Italy, recorded in some 300 inscriptions from about the 6th to the 1st century BCE.</dd></div><div id="verhansung"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/verhansung">Verhansung</a></dt><dd>Verhansung was the expulsion of a town from the Hanseatic League, the gravest sanction the Hansa could impose on a disobedient member.</dd></div><div id="vietnamese-alphabet"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/vietnamese-alphabet">Vietnamese alphabet</a> <span class="gl-aka">(chữ Quốc ngữ, Quốc ngữ)</span></dt><dd>The Vietnamese alphabet, chữ Quốc ngữ, is the Latin-based script used to write Vietnamese, with diacritics marking tones and vowels.</dd></div><div id="vikram-samvat"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/vikram-samvat">Vikram Samvat</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Vikrama Samvat)</span></dt><dd>Vikram Samvat is a Hindu calendar era that begins in 57 BCE, still widely used in India and the official calendar of Nepal.</dd></div><div id="vilayet"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/vilayet">Vilayet</a></dt><dd>A vilayet was a first-level province of the Ottoman Empire under the administrative system introduced by the Provincial Law of 1864, which replaced the older eyalets.</dd></div><div id="villa-estate"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/villa-estate">Villa (estate)</a> <span class="gl-aka">(villae)</span></dt><dd>A villa, in the Carolingian context, was a great landed estate belonging to the church, the crown or the aristocracy.</dd></div><div id="villein"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/villein">Villein</a></dt><dd>A villein was a serf of medieval western Europe, tied to the land of a manor, who owed its lord dues and services, including work on his fields, and could not leave without his permission.</dd></div><div id="vogt"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/vogt">Vogt</a> <span class="gl-aka">(advocatus)</span></dt><dd>Vogt (plural Vögte), from the Latin advocatus, was a German title for an official who protected and represented church lands and later, more generally, for a lord's or a king's administrator of a district, with judicial and fiscal powers.</dd></div><div id="volga-trade-route"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/volga-trade-route">Volga trade route</a></dt><dd>The Volga trade route was a network of river and portage routes linking Scandinavia and the Baltic with the Caspian Sea and the Islamic world via the Volga River in the early medieval period.</dd></div><div id="voltaic-pile"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/voltaic-pile">Voltaic pile</a></dt><dd>The voltaic pile is a stack of paired metal discs separated by brine-soaked cloth or cardboard, the first electrical battery that could continuously provide an electric current; it was invented by the Italian chemist Alessandro Volta, who…</dd></div><div id="vorort"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/vorort">Vorort</a></dt><dd>The Vorort was the leading or presiding canton of the Swiss Confederation before 1848, which convened the federal Diet and conducted common business between sessions.</dd></div><div id="vunivalu-of-bau"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/vunivalu-of-bau">Vunivalu of Bau</a></dt><dd>The Vunivalu of Bau is the paramount chiefly title of Bau in Fiji, originally that of the war king, who shared authority with the senior sacred chief, the Roko Tui Bau.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-w"><h2>W</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="wa-na"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/wa-na">Wa Na</a></dt><dd>Wa Na is the title of the paramount chief of Wa, a Wala kingdom in the Upper West Region of Ghana.</dd></div><div id="wagshum"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/wagshum">Wagshum</a></dt><dd>Wagshum was the hereditary title of the rulers of Wag, a district of Lasta in northern Ethiopia, whose family claimed descent from the Zagwe dynasty.</dd></div><div id="war-elephant"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">War elephant</a> <span class="gl-aka">(war elephants)</span></dt><dd>A war elephant is an elephant trained for combat, used to charge enemy formations, carry archers and intimidate opponents.</dd></div><div id="war-reparations"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">War reparations</a> <span class="gl-aka">(reparations)</span></dt><dd>War reparations are payments imposed on a defeated state to compensate the victors for the costs and damage of war.</dd></div><div id="warring-states-period"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a></dt><dd>The Warring States period was the final phase of the Eastern Zhou in ancient China, when seven major states, Qin, Chu, Qi, Yan, Han, Zhao and Wei, fought for supremacy.</dd></div><div id="water-wheel"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/water-wheel">Water wheel</a> <span class="gl-aka">(waterwheel)</span></dt><dd>A water wheel is a machine that turns the energy of flowing or falling water into power through a large wheel with blades or buckets on its rim; it first appeared in ancient Egypt in the 4th century BCE, where wheels lifted water for…</dd></div><div id="well"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/well">Well</a> <span class="gl-aka">(wells)</span></dt><dd>A well is a shaft dug into the ground to reach water, essential for life and travel in arid regions such as the Sahara and the Sahel.</dd></div><div id="welshry"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/welshry">Welshry</a></dt><dd>The Welshry was the part of a Marcher lordship in medieval Wales where native Welsh tenants lived under Welsh law and custom, as opposed to the Englishry, where settlers held land under English law.</dd></div><div id="weser-renaissance"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/weser-renaissance">Weser Renaissance</a></dt><dd>Weser Renaissance is the style of Renaissance architecture in the Weser region of north-western Germany, flourishing from about 1520 to 1620.</dd></div><div id="west-indies-associated-states"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/west-indies-associated-states">West Indies Associated States</a></dt><dd>The West Indies Associated States was the collective name for British colonies in the eastern Caribbean that became states in free association with the United Kingdom under the West Indies Act 1967.</dd></div><div id="wet-rice-agriculture"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/wet-rice-agriculture">Wet-rice agriculture</a></dt><dd>Wet-rice agriculture is the cultivation of rice in flooded paddy fields, a highly productive system that can support dense populations.</dd></div><div id="wheel"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/wheel">Wheel</a></dt><dd>A wheel is a rotating component, usually circular, that turns on an axle; the oldest indirect evidence of wheeled movement, miniature clay wheels found north of the Black Sea, dates from before 4000 BCE.</dd></div><div id="windmill"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/windmill">Windmill</a></dt><dd>A windmill is a machine driven by wind acting on vanes or sails to grind grain or pump water, first known in Persia, where windmills were working in Khorasan in the 9th century.</dd></div><div id="wohler-synthesis"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/wohler-synthesis">Wöhler synthesis</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Wohler synthesis)</span></dt><dd>The Wöhler synthesis is the conversion of ammonium cyanate into urea, described by Friedrich Wöhler in 1828 and often cited as the starting point of modern organic chemistry.</dd></div><div id="woodblock-printing"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/woodblock-printing">Woodblock printing</a> <span class="gl-aka">(wood-block printing)</span></dt><dd>Woodblock printing is a technique of printing text or images by carving them in relief on a wooden block, inking it and pressing paper onto it.</dd></div><div id="world-wide-web"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/world-wide-web">World Wide Web</a> <span class="gl-aka">(WWW)</span></dt><dd>The World Wide Web is a global system of linked documents and other resources shared over the Internet, invented at CERN in 1989 by the English computer scientist Tim Berners-Lee.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-x"><h2>X</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="x-ray"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/x-ray">X-ray</a> <span class="gl-aka">(X-radiation, Röntgen rays)</span></dt><dd>An X-ray is a form of high-energy electromagnetic radiation, with a wavelength shorter than ultraviolet rays and longer than gamma rays, discovered by the German physicist Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen on 8 November 1895.</dd></div><div id="xing-ware"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/xing-ware">Xing ware</a></dt><dd>Xing ware is a type of white porcelain produced at kilns in northern China under the Tang Dynasty.</dd></div><div id="xuanming-calendar"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/xuanming-calendar">Xuanming calendar</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Senmyō calendar, Senmyō-reki)</span></dt><dd>The Xuanming calendar was an astronomical calendar compiled in Tang China and adopted in 822.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-y"><h2>Y</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="ya-na"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ya-na">Ya Na</a></dt><dd>Ya Na is the title of the king of Dagbon, the kingdom of the Dagomba in northern Ghana, who reigns from Yendi.</dd></div><div id="yang-di-pertuan-agong"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/yang-di-pertuan-agong">Yang di-Pertuan Agong</a> <span class="gl-aka">(King of Malaysia)</span></dt><dd>The Yang di-Pertuan Agong is the head of state of Malaysia, a constitutional monarch elected for a five-year term by the Conference of Rulers from among the nine hereditary rulers of the Malay states.</dd></div><div id="yang-dipertuan-muda"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/yang-dipertuan-muda">Yang Dipertuan Muda</a></dt><dd>Yang Dipertuan Muda ('junior ruler') was the title of the viceroy or under-king of the Johor-Riau and later Riau-Lingga sultanates.</dd></div><div id="yavana"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/yavana">Yavana</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Yavanas)</span></dt><dd>Yavana is a term used in ancient Indian languages for Greeks and, later, for other westerners.</dd></div><div id="year-name"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/year-name">Year-name</a> <span class="gl-aka">(year-names)</span></dt><dd>A year-name was a dating formula used in early Mesopotamia, in which each year was named after a notable event of the previous year, such as a military victory or the building of a temple.</dd></div><div id="yemsa-language"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/yemsa-language">Yemsa language</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Janjero language)</span></dt><dd>Yemsa, formerly called Janjero, is an Omotic language of south-western Ethiopia, spoken by the Yem people in the hills east of the Omo river.</dd></div><div id="yerba-mate"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/yerba-mate">Yerba mate</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Ilex paraguariensis)</span></dt><dd>Yerba mate is a holly, Ilex paraguariensis, native to the forests of Paraguay, southern Brazil and north-eastern Argentina, whose dried leaves are steeped to make the caffeinated drink mate.</dd></div><div id="yiddish"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a></dt><dd>Yiddish is a language of the Ashkenazi Jews, based on medieval German with Hebrew, Aramaic and Slavic elements and written in the Hebrew alphabet.</dd></div><div id="yuris-day"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/yuris-day">Yuri's Day</a> <span class="gl-aka">(St George's Day (Russia))</span></dt><dd>Yuri's Day, the autumn feast of St George on 26 November, was the time when Russian peasants were allowed to leave their landlords.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="gl-letter" id="gl-z"><h2>Z</h2><dl class="gl-list"><div id="zaju"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/zaju">Zaju</a></dt><dd>Zaju is a form of Chinese drama combining spoken dialogue, song and music, which reached its classical form under the Yuan Dynasty.</dd></div><div id="zamindar"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">Zamindar</a></dt><dd>A zamindar was the holder of an estate, a zamindari, in the Indian subcontinent, typically hereditary and with the right to collect taxes; the Persian word for landowner came into use under the Mughal Empire.</dd></div><div id="zamorin"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorin</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Samoothiri)</span></dt><dd>The Zamorin, in Malayalam Samoothiri, was the title of the Hindu rulers of the Kingdom of Calicut on the Malabar Coast of Kerala from about the 12th century to 1766.</dd></div><div id="zasagt-khan"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/zasagt-khan">Zasagt Khan</a></dt><dd>Zasagt Khan was the title of the Chinggisid rulers of the western Khalkha Mongols, one of the three, later four, Khalkha khanates.</dd></div><div id="zb-vz-26"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/zb-vz-26">ZB vz. 26</a></dt><dd>The ZB vz. 26 was a light machine gun designed by Václav Holek at the Zbrojovka Brno arms works and adopted by the Czechoslovak army in 1926.</dd></div><div id="zenden"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/zenden">Zenden</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Dizains, Seven Zenden)</span></dt><dd>The Zenden (French dizains) were the self-governing communal districts of the Upper Valais, in present-day Switzerland.</dd></div><div id="ziggurat"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">Ziggurat</a></dt><dd>A ziggurat is a massive stepped temple tower built in ancient Mesopotamia and Elam, with a shrine on its summit.</dd></div><div id="zugewandter-ort"><dt><a href="/encyclopedia/zugewandter-ort">Zugewandter Ort</a> <span class="gl-aka">(Associate of the Swiss Confederacy)</span></dt><dd>A Zugewandter Ort (associated place) was a territory allied with the Old Swiss Confederacy without being a full member canton.</dd></div></dl></section>
